                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 65-year-old man with a history of osteoporosis, dyslipidemia, and diabetes. He presents to the emergency department with gradually worsening back pain that seems to have worsened after moving furniture the other day. He also reports numbness and weakness in his legs while walking, which improves when he is riding his bike or pushing a cart at the grocery store. The patient drinks 3 alcoholic drinks every day and has a 44 pack-year smoking history. His vital signs are notable for hypertension. Physical exam is notable for a non-tender spine with normal mobility in all 4 directions. Radiography of the spine and basic labs are ordered.\n\nThe most likely diagnosis is spinal stenosis. Spinal stenosis is a narrowing of the spinal canal that can cause compression of the spinal cord or nerve roots. It is more common in older adults and can be caused by degenerative changes in the spine, such as osteoarthritis or herniated discs. The patient's history of osteoporosis and dyslipidemia may contribute to the development of spinal stenosis. The patient's symptoms of numbness and weakness in the legs while walking, which improve with biking or pushing a cart, are consistent with neurogenic claudication, a common symptom of spinal stenosis. The patient's hypertension may also be a contributing factor to the development of spinal stenosis. The non-tender spine with normal mobility in all 4 directions on physical exam is not consistent with a musculoskeletal strain or herniated nucleus pulposus. Vascular claudication is less likely given the patient's history and symptoms. Radiography of the spine and basic labs will help confirm the diagnosis and rule out other potential causes of back pain.",
                        "answers": [
