                        "cot": "\n\nB) Membrano proliferative glomerulonephritis (MPGN) is the most likely cause based on the clinical presentation and biopsy findings. MPGN is characterized by hypercellular glomeruli with electron dense deposits along the glomerular basement membrane, which can lead to proteinuria, hematuria, and decreased kidney function. The presence of C3 nephritic factor, a type of autoantibody that stabilizes the alternative complement pathway, is also suggestive of MPGN. PSGN typically presents with a history of recent streptococcal infection, while RPGN is characterized by rapidly declining kidney function over days to weeks. Minimal change disease and membranous glomerulonephritis typically do not present with hypercellularity on biopsy.",
