                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient presents with tiredness and lethargy, and investigations show low hemoglobin and high serum creatinine levels. The urinalysis shows proteinuria, and the renal biopsy demonstrates hypercellular glomerulus with electron dense deposits along the glomerular basement membrane. The serum is negative for ANA and positive for C3 nephritic factor. \n\nBased on these findings, the most likely cause is Membrano proliferative glomerulonephritis (MPGN). MPGN is characterized by thickening of the glomerular basement membrane due to the deposition of immune complexes, resulting in decreased kidney function and proteinuria. The presence of C3 nephritic factor is also suggestive of MPGN. \n\nPoststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) is characterized by a recent history of streptococcal infection, which is not mentioned in the case. Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN) is characterized by a rapid decline in kidney function, which is not mentioned in the case. Minimal change disease is characterized by normal appearing glomeruli on biopsy, which is not the case here. Membranous glomerulonephritis (MGN) is characterized by thickening of the glomerular basement membrane due to immune complex deposition, but it does not typically present with hypercellularity on biopsy. \n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of the patient's symptoms and findings is Membrano proliferative glomerulonephritis (MPGN).",
