                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient has a history of myocardial infarction, leg swelling, right upper abdominal tenderness, fatigue, syncope, smoking, and occasional alcohol consumption. On physical examination, the patient has visible jugular vein distention, bilateral lower leg pitting edema, occasional bilateral wheezes, decreased S1, S3 gallop, grade 3/6 systolic murmur, ascites, and positive hepatojugular reflux.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is an echocardiogram?\n2. What are the different clinical findings that can be observed on an echocardiogram?\n3. Which clinical finding is most likely to be observed in this patient based on his history and physical examination?\n\nAnswers:\n1. An echocardiogram is a non-invasive test that uses sound waves to create images of the heart.\n2. Clinical findings that can be observed on an echocardiogram include left ventricular ejection fraction, tricuspid regurgitation, wall motion abnormalities, left ventricular relaxation, and right atrial pressure.\n3. Based on the patient's history and physical examination, the most likely clinical finding observed on an echocardiogram would be increased peak tricuspid regurgitation (B). This is because the patient has signs of right-sided heart failure, including jugular vein distention, lower leg edema, and positive hepatojugular reflux. Tricuspid regurgitation is a common finding in right-sided heart failure and can be detected on an echocardiogram. The other options (A, C, D, and E) are less likely based on the patient's presentation.",
