                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nE) Infundibulopelvic ligament\n\nThe infundibulopelvic ligament (also known as the suspensory ligament of the ovary) contains the ovarian artery and vein, as well as nerves and lymphatics. In this case, the lack of flow in the right ovarian vein suggests a possible ovarian torsion, which can occur when the ovary twists on its pedicle, compromising blood flow. The infundibulopelvic ligament is the most likely ligament to be involved in ovarian torsion. The other ligaments listed do not contain the ovarian vessels and are less likely to be involved in this scenario.",
