                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient has sudden-onset, severe right flank pain and tenderness in the right lower quadrant with guarding. Pelvic ultrasound shows a large simple cyst on the right ovary, and Doppler shows no flow in the right ovarian vein. This suggests ovarian torsion, which is a twisting of the ovary and its vascular pedicle, leading to compromised blood flow and ischemia.\n\nThe ligament most likely to be involved in ovarian torsion is the infundibulopelvic ligament, also known as the suspensory ligament of the ovary. This ligament contains the ovarian artery and vein, as well as nerves and lymphatics, and attaches the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall. Twisting of the ovary can cause torsion of the infundibulopelvic ligament, leading to compromised blood flow and ischemia.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E) Infundibulopelvic ligament.",
