                    {
                        "id": "a6a41784-db00-40e9-ae80-799972678d63",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-01",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 36-year-old woman with a history of recurrent ovarian cysts who presents with sudden-onset, severe right flank pain. Abdominal examination shows tenderness in the right lower quadrant with guarding. Pelvic ultrasound shows a large simple cyst on the right ovary, and Doppler shows detectable flow in the right ovarian artery but no flow in the right ovarian vein. \n\nThis presentation is consistent with ovarian torsion, which occurs when the ovary twists on its pedicle, compromising blood flow to the ovary. The lack of flow in the right ovarian vein suggests that the torsion is affecting the venous drainage of the ovary. \n\nTo determine which ligament is most likely involved, it is important to understand the anatomy of the ovary and its supporting structures. The ovary is suspended in the pelvis by several ligaments, including the broad ligament, round ligament of the uterus, ovarian ligament, cardinal ligament, and infundibulopelvic ligament. \n\nThe broad ligament is a sheet of peritoneum that attaches to the sides of the uterus and extends laterally to the pelvic sidewalls. It contains the fallopian tubes, ovaries, and other structures. The round ligament of the uterus runs from the uterine fundus to the labia majora and helps to support the uterus. The ovarian ligament attaches the ovary to the uterus. The cardinal ligament is a thick band of connective tissue that runs from the cervix to the pelvic sidewalls and contains the uterine artery, uterine vein, and other structures. The infundibulopelvic ligament (also known as the suspensory ligament of the ovary) attaches the ovary to the pelvic sidewall and contains the ovarian artery, ovarian vein, and other structures. \n\nIn ovarian torsion, the ovary twists on its pedicle, which can cause compression of the blood vessels and compromise blood flow to the ovary. The infundibulopelvic ligament contains the ovarian vessels, so it is the most likely ligament to be involved in ovarian torsion. Therefore, the correct answer is E) Infundibulopelvic ligament.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "dcd08cf6-d521-428c-9621-68314bf8ed72",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "E) Infundibulopelvic ligament.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "E",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
