                        "cot": "Step 1: Let's work out every answer option:\nA) Broad ligament: The broad ligament supports the uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries. It is unlikely to be involved in this case as the pain is localized to the right flank and there is a large cyst on the right ovary.\nB) Round ligament of the uterus: The round ligament of the uterus supports the uterus and runs from the uterine horns to the labia majora. It is unlikely to be involved in this case as the pain is not in the pelvic region.\nC) Ovarian ligament: The ovarian ligament attaches the ovary to the uterus. It is possible that the ovarian ligament could be involved in this case as there is a large cyst on the right ovary.\nD) Cardinal ligament: The cardinal ligament supports the cervix and upper vagina. It is unlikely to be involved in this case as the pain is not in the pelvic region.\nE) Infundibulopelvic ligament: The infundibulopelvic ligament supports the ovary and runs from the ovary to the pelvic wall. It is possible that the infundibulopelvic ligament could be involved in this case as there is a large cyst on the right ovary.\n\nStep 2: Combining all the information, it is most likely that the ovarian ligament or infundibulopelvic ligament is involved in this case as there is a large cyst on the right ovary and no flow detected in the right ovarian vein.\n\nStep 3: The answer is either C) Ovarian ligament or E) Infundibulopelvic ligament. Both ligaments are involved in supporting the ovary, but the infundibulopelvic ligament is more specifically involved in attaching the ovary to the pelvic wall. Therefore, the best answer is E) Infundibulopelvic ligament.",
