                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient presents with sudden-onset, severe right flank pain and tenderness in the right lower quadrant with guarding. Pelvic ultrasound shows a large simple cyst on the right ovary, with detectable flow in the right ovarian artery but no flow in the right ovarian vein. Based on this information, the most likely diagnosis is ovarian torsion, which occurs when the ovary twists on its pedicle, compromising blood flow and causing ischemia and pain. \n\nTo determine which ligament is most likely involved, we need to consider the anatomy of the ovary and its supporting structures. The ovary is suspended in the pelvic cavity by several ligaments, including the broad ligament, ovarian ligament, and infundibulopelvic ligament (also known as the suspensory ligament). The round ligament of the uterus and cardinal ligament are not directly involved in supporting the ovary.\n\nOf the three ligaments that support the ovary, the infundibulopelvic ligament is the most likely to be involved in ovarian torsion. This ligament contains the ovarian artery, vein, and nerves, which are all at risk of torsion when the ovary twists. The broad ligament and ovarian ligament are less likely to be involved in ovarian torsion, as they do not contain the major blood vessels and nerves that supply the ovary.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E) Infundibulopelvic ligament.",
