                        "cot": "Rephrased question: Which ligament is most likely involved in a 36-year-old woman with a history of recurrent ovarian cysts, sudden-onset severe right flank pain, tenderness in the right lower quadrant with guarding, and a large simple cyst on the right ovary with detectable right ovarian artery flow but no flow detected in the right ovarian vein?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The patient has a large simple cyst on the right ovary, which is causing the pain and tenderness in the right lower quadrant with guarding.\n- The Doppler ultrasound shows detectable right ovarian artery flow but no flow detected in the right ovarian vein.\n- This suggests that there is a problem with venous drainage from the right ovary.\n- The ligament that is most likely involved in this problem is the one that provides venous drainage from the right ovary.\n- The infundibulopelvic ligament (also known as the suspensory ligament of the ovary) is the ligament that contains the ovarian vessels, including the ovarian vein, and provides venous drainage from the ovary.\n- Therefore, the infundibulopelvic ligament is the most likely ligament to have been involved in this case.\n\nAnswer: E) Infundibulopelvic ligament",
