                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The ovaries are located in the pelvic cavity and are supported by several ligaments, including the broad ligament, round ligament of the uterus, ovarian ligament, cardinal ligament, and infundibulopelvic ligament. Ovarian cysts are fluid-filled sacs that can develop on or within the ovaries. They are common and usually benign, but can cause pain and other symptoms if they rupture or become twisted.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the most likely cause of the patient's sudden-onset, severe right flank pain?\nAnswer: The large simple cyst on the right ovary seen on pelvic ultrasound is the most likely cause of the patient's pain.\n\n2. What is the significance of the Doppler findings in the right ovarian artery and vein?\nAnswer: The detectable flow in the right ovarian artery suggests that there is still blood supply to the ovary, while the lack of flow in the right ovarian vein suggests that there may be venous obstruction or thrombosis.\n\n3. Which ligament(s) could be involved in the patient's condition?\nAnswer: The ligaments that support the ovary are the broad ligament, round ligament of the uterus, ovarian ligament, cardinal ligament, and infundibulopelvic ligament. Any of these ligaments could potentially be involved in the patient's condition.\n\nAnswer: Based on the patient's symptoms and imaging findings, the most likely diagnosis is ovarian torsion, which occurs when the ovary twists on its supporting ligaments, leading to compromised blood flow and ischemia. The infundibulopelvic ligament, which connects the ovary to the pelvic sidewall, is the most common site of torsion. Therefore, the correct answer is E) Infundibulopelvic ligament.",
