                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, can you explain the case presented in the question?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the case is about a 36-year-old woman who came to the emergency department because of sudden-onset, severe right flank pain. She has a history of recurrent ovarian cysts and her pelvic ultrasound shows a large simple cyst on the right ovary. Right ovarian artery flow is detectable on Doppler, but there is no flow detected in the right ovarian vein.\n\nStudent: What does it mean that there is no flow detected in the right ovarian vein?\n\nProfessor: It means that there is a blockage in the right ovarian vein, which can cause the blood to back up and lead to swelling and pain in the area.\n\nStudent: Which of the following ligaments is most likely to have been involved?\n\nProfessor: The most likely ligament to have been involved in this case is the infundibulopelvic ligament. This ligament connects the ovary to the pelvic wall and contains the ovarian vessels, including the ovarian artery and vein. A blockage in the right ovarian vein could have occurred in this ligament, leading to the symptoms described in the case.\n\nStudent: What about the other ligaments listed in the answer choices?\n\nProfessor: The broad ligament is a sheet-like structure that supports the uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries. The round ligament of the uterus connects the uterus to the labia majora and helps to support the uterus during pregnancy. The ovarian ligament connects the ovary to the uterus. The cardinal ligament provides support to the cervix and helps to anchor the uterus in place. None of these ligaments are directly involved in the blood supply to the ovary, so they are less likely to be involved in this case.",
