                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the most likely cause of cyanosis in a newborn boy who showed immediate improvement in tachypnea and oxygenation status after being started on an inhaled medication that increases smooth muscle cGMP, but later developed blue-grey discoloration of the lips, fingers, and toes?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The newborn boy was evaluated for tachypnea six hours after birth.\n- He was delivered at 41 weeks' gestation via Caesarian section and the amniotic fluid was meconium-stained.\n- His respiratory rate is 75/min and physical examination shows increased work of breathing.\n- X-rays of the abdomen and chest show no abnormalities.\n- Echocardiography shows elevated pulmonary artery pressure.\n- He is started on an inhaled medication that increases smooth muscle cGMP, and there is immediate improvement in his tachypnea and oxygenation status.\n- Three hours later, the newborn is tachypneic and there is blue-grey discoloration of the lips, fingers, and toes.\n- The question asks for the most likely cause of this infant's cyanosis.\n\nAnswer: B) Closure of the ductus arteriosus is the most likely cause of this infant's cyanosis. The inhaled medication that increases smooth muscle cGMP likely caused the closure of the ductus arteriosus, which is a normal process after birth but can cause cyanosis if it happens too quickly or too early. The other options are not directly related to the symptoms described in the scenario.",
                        "answers": [
