                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what tachypnea is?\n\nProfessor: Tachypnea is a medical term that refers to rapid breathing. In this case, the newborn boy is breathing at a rate of 75 breaths per minute, which is faster than normal.\n\nStudent: What does it mean that the amniotic fluid was meconium-stained?\n\nProfessor: Meconium is the first stool that a newborn passes after birth. If the amniotic fluid is meconium-stained, it means that the baby passed stool before delivery, which can be a sign of fetal distress.\n\nStudent: What is elevated pulmonary artery pressure?\n\nProfessor: Pulmonary artery pressure is the pressure in the blood vessels that carry blood from the heart to the lungs. Elevated pulmonary artery pressure means that the pressure in these blood vessels is higher than normal.\n\nStudent: What is the inhaled medication that increases smooth muscle cGMP?\n\nProfessor: The medication is likely to be a type of bronchodilator that relaxes the smooth muscles in the airways and increases the levels of cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP), which helps to dilate the blood vessels in the lungs.\n\nStudent: What is cyanosis?\n\nProfessor: Cyanosis is a medical term that refers to a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes, which is caused by a lack of oxygen in the blood.\n\nStudent: What is the most likely cause of the infant's cyanosis?\n\nProfessor: The most likely cause of the infant's cyanosis is closure of the ductus arteriosus. The ductus arteriosus is a blood vessel that connects the pulmonary artery to the aorta in the fetus. It normally closes shortly after birth, but in some cases, it can remain open, causing blood to bypass the lungs and leading to a lack of oxygen in the blood.",
