                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the antibiotic used in the experiment (ciprofloxacin).\n2. Determine the mechanism of action of ciprofloxacin (inhibits DNA gyrase, an enzyme involved in DNA replication).\n3. Consider the options for antibiotic resistance mechanisms:\nA) Replacement of D-alanine: This mechanism is associated with resistance to vancomycin, not ciprofloxacin.\nB) Mutation of DNA gyrase: This is a possible mechanism of resistance to ciprofloxacin, as a mutation in the DNA gyrase gene could prevent the antibiotic from binding to the enzyme.\nC) Inactivation by bacterial transferase: This mechanism is associated with resistance to aminoglycosides, not ciprofloxacin.\nD) Production of \u03b2-lactamase: This mechanism is associated with resistance to \u03b2-lactam antibiotics, such as penicillin, not ciprofloxacin.\nE) Modification of surface binding proteins: This mechanism is associated with resistance to tetracyclines, not ciprofloxacin.\n\nTherefore, the most likely mechanism of antibiotic resistance in the surviving colony is B) Mutation of DNA gyrase.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. The antibiotic used in the experiment is ciprofloxacin.\n2. Ciprofloxacin inhibits DNA gyrase, an enzyme involved in DNA replication.\n3. Option A is not relevant to ciprofloxacin. Option B is a possible mechanism of resistance to ciprofloxacin. Option C is not relevant to ciprofloxacin. Option D is not relevant to ciprofloxacin. Option E is not relevant to ciprofloxacin. Therefore, the most likely mechanism of antibiotic resistance in the surviving colony is B) Mutation of DNA gyrase.",
