                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nDifficult. \n\nStep-by-step reasoning: \n\nEscherichia coli is a type of bacteria that can cause infections in humans. Antibiotics are often used to treat these infections, but overuse and misuse of antibiotics can lead to the development of antibiotic-resistant bacteria. In this scenario, the microbiologist has isolated several colonies of Escherichia coli on an agar plate and exposed the plate to ciprofloxacin, an antibiotic that is commonly used to treat bacterial infections. Ciprofloxacin works by inhibiting the activity of DNA gyrase, an enzyme that is essential for bacterial DNA replication. \n\nHowever, one colony of Escherichia coli survives the exposure to ciprofloxacin. This suggests that the surviving colony has developed a mechanism of antibiotic resistance. The question asks which of the following is the most likely mechanism of antibiotic resistance in the surviving colony. \n\nA) Replacement of D-alanine: This is not a known mechanism of antibiotic resistance in Escherichia coli. \n\nB) Mutation of DNA gyrase: This is a possible mechanism of antibiotic resistance in Escherichia coli. If the bacteria mutate their DNA gyrase enzyme, they may be able to continue replicating their DNA even in the presence of ciprofloxacin. \n\nC) Inactivation by bacterial transferase: This is not a known mechanism of antibiotic resistance in Escherichia coli. \n\nD) Production of \u03b2-lactamase: This is not a likely mechanism of antibiotic resistance in Escherichia coli in this scenario, as ciprofloxacin is not a \u03b2-lactam antibiotic. \n\nE) Modification of surface binding proteins: This is not a known mechanism of antibiotic resistance in Escherichia coli. \n\nTherefore, the most likely mechanism of antibiotic resistance in the surviving colony is B) Mutation of DNA gyrase.",
