                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nDifficult. The patient is presenting with symptoms of shortness of breath, cough, and difficulty breathing, along with a history of a previous heart attack and alcohol consumption. The next step in management would depend on the results of the ECG and chest X-ray. Therefore, a step-by-step reasoning approach is needed.\n\nThe ECG and chest X-ray are important diagnostic tests that can help identify the cause of the patient's symptoms. The ECG can show any abnormalities in the heart's electrical activity, while the chest X-ray can show any abnormalities in the lungs or heart.\n\nIf the ECG and chest X-ray suggest that the patient is experiencing heart failure, then an echocardiogram may be the next step in management. An echocardiogram is a non-invasive test that uses sound waves to create images of the heart. It can help identify any structural abnormalities in the heart that may be causing heart failure.\n\nIf the ECG and chest X-ray suggest that the patient is experiencing a pulmonary embolism or other lung-related issue, then a CT scan may be the next step in management. A CT scan can help identify any blood clots or other abnormalities in the lungs.\n\nIf the ECG and chest X-ray are inconclusive, then a stress test may be the next step in management. A stress test involves exercising on a treadmill or stationary bike while being monitored for changes in heart rate and blood pressure. It can help identify any abnormalities in the heart's function that may be causing the patient's symptoms.\n\nTherefore, the answer is not A, B, E, but either C or D, depending on the results of the ECG and chest X-ray.",
                        "answers": [
