                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The correct answer is A) Defect in WASP gene.\nThe most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is a defect in the WASP gene, which is associated with the autosomal recessive disorder, Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome (WAS). This syndrome is characterized by immunodeficiency, eczema, and susceptibility to infections. The most common finding is thrombocytopenia, which occurs in nearly all patients. Other common features include recurrent infections, especially viral infections, and autoimmune disorders. Patients may also have lymphadenopathy and an increased risk of developing lymphoma or leukemia.\nWiskott-Aldrich syndrome is caused by mutations in the WASP gene, which codes for the WASP protein, a member of the Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein family. This protein plays a role in cell division, cell cycle progression, and actin polymerization. The WASP protein is also involved in the activation of the immune system, and mutations in the WASP gene lead to the immune dysfunction seen in patients with Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome.",
