                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the patient's symptoms: failure to gain weight, chronic diarrhea, multiple bacterial and viral infections, absent thymic shadow, absence of germinal centers in lymph node biopsy.\n2. Determine which genetic disorders are associated with these symptoms.\n3. Evaluate each answer choice to determine which genetic disorder is most likely the cause of the patient's symptoms.\n\nGoing through each answer choice:\nA) Defect in WASP gene: This gene is associated with Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, which presents with eczema, thrombocytopenia, and recurrent infections. While recurrent infections are a symptom shared with the patient, the other symptoms do not fit with this diagnosis. Therefore, this answer choice is unlikely.\nB) Defect in ATM gene: This gene is associated with ataxia-telangiectasia, which presents with ataxia, telangiectasias, and increased risk of cancer. None of these symptoms fit with the patient's presentation, so this answer choice is unlikely.\nC) Adenosine deaminase deficiency: This deficiency leads to severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID), which presents with recurrent infections and failure to thrive. These symptoms fit with the patient's presentation, so this answer choice is a possibility.\nD) NADPH oxidase deficiency: This deficiency leads to chronic granulomatous disease (CGD), which presents with recurrent infections and granuloma formation. While recurrent infections are a symptom shared with the patient, granuloma formation is not mentioned in the patient's presentation. Therefore, this answer choice is unlikely.\nE) Defect in BTK gene: This gene is associated with X-linked agammaglobulinemia, which presents with recurrent bacterial infections and absent B cells. While recurrent infections are a symptom shared with the patient, the absence of germinal centers in the lymph node biopsy suggests a more severe immunodeficiency than just absent B cells. Therefore, this answer choice is unlikely.\n\nBased on this evaluation, the most likely cause of the patient's symptoms is C) Adenosine deaminase deficiency.",
