                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Defect in WASP gene: This gene is associated with Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, which presents with eczema, thrombocytopenia, and recurrent infections. While some of the symptoms match, the absence of germinal centers and thymic shadow are not typical of this syndrome. \n\nB) Defect in ATM gene: This gene is associated with ataxia-telangiectasia, which presents with ataxia, telangiectasias, and an increased risk of cancer. None of these symptoms match the patient's presentation. \n\nC) Adenosine deaminase deficiency: This deficiency is associated with severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID), which presents with recurrent infections and failure to thrive. However, the absence of germinal centers and thymic shadow are not typical of this deficiency. \n\nD) NADPH oxidase deficiency: This deficiency is associated with chronic granulomatous disease (CGD), which presents with recurrent infections and granuloma formation. However, the absence of germinal centers and thymic shadow are not typical of this deficiency. \n\nE) Defect in BTK gene: This gene is associated with X-linked agammaglobulinemia (XLA), which presents with recurrent bacterial infections and absent B cells. The absence of germinal centers and thymic shadow are typical of this deficiency, as B cells are necessary for germinal center formation and T cell maturation in the thymus. \n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is E) Defect in BTK gene.",
