                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the most likely cause of the 2-month-old girl's symptoms, including failure to gain weight, chronic diarrhea, multiple infections, absent thymic shadow, and absence of germinal centers in lymph node biopsy?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The symptoms suggest an immunodeficiency disorder, as the patient is experiencing chronic infections and has an absent thymic shadow and absence of germinal centers in lymph node biopsy.\n- The options provided are all related to different types of immunodeficiency disorders.\n- A) Defect in WASP gene: This gene is associated with Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, which is characterized by eczema, thrombocytopenia, and recurrent infections. While recurrent infections are present in this patient, there is no mention of eczema or thrombocytopenia, so this option is unlikely.\n- B) Defect in ATM gene: This gene is associated with ataxia-telangiectasia, which is characterized by progressive cerebellar ataxia, oculocutaneous telangiectasias, and recurrent infections. While recurrent infections are present in this patient, there is no mention of cerebellar ataxia or telangiectasias, so this option is unlikely.\n- C) Adenosine deaminase deficiency: This is a type of severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) that is characterized by recurrent infections, failure to thrive, and absent thymic shadow. This option is a possibility, but we need to consider the other options as well.\n- D) NADPH oxidase deficiency: This is a type of chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) that is characterized by recurrent infections, especially with catalase-positive organisms, and granuloma formation. While recurrent infections are present in this patient, there is no mention of granuloma formation, so this option is unlikely.\n- E) Defect in BTK gene: This gene is associated with X-linked agammaglobulinemia, which is characterized by recurrent bacterial infections, absent B cells, and low immunoglobulin levels. While recurrent infections are present in this patient, there is no mention of absent B cells or low immunoglobulin levels, so this option is unlikely.\n- Based on the above reasoning, the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is C) Adenosine deaminase deficiency.\n\nAnswer: C) Adenosine deaminase deficiency.",
