                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nDifficult. \n\nThe symptoms described in the question suggest a possible immunodeficiency disorder. The absence of thymic shadow and germinal centers further support this possibility. To answer the question, we need to identify the immunodeficiency disorder that is most likely to cause these symptoms. \n\nOption A refers to Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, which is characterized by eczema, thrombocytopenia, and recurrent infections. It is caused by a defect in the WASP gene, which is involved in cytoskeletal organization and signaling in immune cells. This disorder is not typically associated with chronic diarrhea. \n\nOption B refers to ataxia-telangiectasia, which is characterized by progressive cerebellar ataxia, telangiectasias, and increased susceptibility to infections and cancer. It is caused by a defect in the ATM gene, which is involved in DNA repair and cell cycle control. This disorder is not typically associated with chronic diarrhea. \n\nOption C refers to adenosine deaminase deficiency, which is characterized by severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID), a condition in which affected individuals have no functional T or B cells and are highly susceptible to infections. Chronic diarrhea is a common feature of this disorder. \n\nOption D refers to chronic granulomatous disease, which is characterized by recurrent bacterial and fungal infections due to impaired phagocytic killing by neutrophils and macrophages. It is caused by a defect in the NADPH oxidase enzyme, which generates reactive oxygen species that are important for microbial killing. This disorder is not typically associated with chronic diarrhea. \n\nOption E refers to X-linked agammaglobulinemia, which is characterized by recurrent bacterial infections due to a lack of mature B cells and low levels of immunoglobulins. It is caused by a defect in the BTK gene, which is involved in B cell development and signaling. This disorder is not typically associated with chronic diarrhea. \n\nBased on the symptoms and findings described in the question, the most likely diagnosis is adenosine deaminase deficiency (option C).",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "f9fc1d1c-bfc4-412c-b574-b221b83e82fe",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Adenosine deaminase deficiency.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
