                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with primary amenorrhea, which means she has not yet had her first menstrual period. The breast examination shows secondary mound formation, indicating that she has undergone breast development, which is a sign of puberty. However, her pubic hair is sparse and lightly pigmented, indicating delayed or incomplete pubertal development. The abdominal examination shows bilateral firm, nontender inguinal masses, which are likely to be the patient's undescended testes. The pelvic examination shows a blind-ended vaginal pouch, indicating that the patient has a congenital absence of the uterus and vagina, which is known as Mayer-Rokitansky-K\u00fcster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome.\n\nThe most appropriate treatment for this patient's condition is gonadectomy, which involves the surgical removal of the testes. This is necessary because the testes are producing testosterone, which can cause virilization (masculinization) of the patient's body. The patient may also be at risk of developing testicular cancer if the testes are not removed. After gonadectomy, the patient may require hormone replacement therapy to induce puberty and maintain bone health.\n\nSpironolactone therapy is used to treat conditions such as hirsutism (excessive hair growth) and acne, which are caused by excess androgen production. This is not appropriate for this patient, as she has a congenital absence of the uterus and vagina, which is not related to androgen excess.\n\nTestosterone therapy is also not appropriate for this patient, as she already has excess testosterone production from her undescended testes. This would further masculinize her body and increase the risk of developing testicular cancer.\n\nVaginal dilatory therapy is used to treat vaginal agenesis (absence of the vagina) or vaginal stenosis (narrowing of the vagina). This is not appropriate for this patient, as she has a blind-ended vaginal pouch, which cannot be dilated.\n\nPrednisolone therapy is a corticosteroid medication used to treat a variety of conditions, including autoimmune disorders and inflammatory diseases. This is not appropriate for this patient, as she does not have any of these conditions.",
