                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what a thrombus is?\n\nProfessor: A thrombus is a blood clot that forms in a blood vessel.\n\nStudent: What is Homan\u2019s sign?\n\nProfessor: Homan\u2019s sign is a test used to check for deep vein thrombosis. It involves dorsiflexing the foot while the knee is extended, which can cause pain in the calf if a thrombus is present.\n\nStudent: What is systemic lupus erythematosus?\n\nProfessor: Systemic lupus erythematosus is an autoimmune disease where the body\u2019s immune system attacks its own tissues and organs.\n\nStudent: What is a mixing study?\n\nProfessor: A mixing study is a test used to determine if a prolonged partial thromboplastin time (PTT) is due to a deficiency in clotting factors or the presence of an inhibitor.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what disseminated intravascular coagulation is?\n\nProfessor: Disseminated intravascular coagulation is a condition where the body\u2019s clotting system becomes overactive and forms clots throughout the body, leading to organ damage and bleeding. It can be caused by a variety of underlying conditions, such as sepsis or cancer. \n\nBased on the patient\u2019s history and lab results, the most likely cause of her condition is B) Antibodies directed against phospholipids, which is a common cause of deep vein thrombosis in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "3077ebbc-f80a-43b4-bbb6-e8671001190e",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
