                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient has a history of asthma and has been experiencing worsening shortness of breath, coughing, and wheezing episodes associated with fever, malaise, and expectoration of greenish sputum and brown mucous plugs. The chest radiograph shows atelectasis in the right lung base and glove-finger shadows, which are suggestive of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA). The serum IgE levels are elevated, which is also consistent with ABPA.\n\nTherefore, the most appropriate therapy in this patient would be antifungal treatment with fluconazole or itraconazole, which are effective in treating ABPA. Isoniazid is used to treat tuberculosis, which is not the likely diagnosis in this patient. Caspofungin is used to treat invasive fungal infections, which are not the likely cause of the patient's symptoms. Prednisone may be used as an adjunctive therapy in ABPA to reduce inflammation, but antifungal treatment is the mainstay of therapy. Surgical resection of the infiltrates is not indicated in ABPA.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Fluconazole.",
