                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient is presenting with fever, nausea, vomiting, and neck stiffness, which are all symptoms commonly associated with meningitis. The CSF results show an elevated cell count, low glucose, and elevated protein, which are consistent with bacterial meningitis.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?\nAnswer: Bacterial meningitis.\n2. What is the appropriate treatment for bacterial meningitis?\nAnswer: Antibiotics.\n3. Which antibiotics are appropriate for treating bacterial meningitis?\nAnswer: Ceftriaxone and vancomycin are the standard antibiotics used to treat bacterial meningitis in adults.\n\nAnswer: D) Ceftriaxone and vancomycin are the best treatment options for this patient's most likely diagnosis of bacterial meningitis. Acyclovir is used to treat viral meningitis, which is less likely given the patient's symptoms and CSF results. Amphotericin and 5-flucytosine are used to treat fungal meningitis, which is also less likely. IV fluids and monitoring are important for supportive care, but antibiotics are necessary to treat the underlying infection. Ceftriaxone alone may be appropriate for treating meningitis caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae, but vancomycin is added to cover for other potential pathogens, such as methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA).",
