                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Betamethasone is a corticosteroid medication that is commonly used to promote fetal lung maturation in cases of preterm labor. It works by stimulating the production of surfactant in the fetal lungs.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the mechanism of action of betamethasone?\n2. What is surfactant and why is it important for fetal lung maturation?\n3. Which fetal cells produce surfactant?\n4. Which answer choice represents the correct fetal cell type that betamethasone stimulates?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Betamethasone is a corticosteroid medication that binds to glucocorticoid receptors in the fetal lung cells, which stimulates the production of surfactant.\n2. Surfactant is a substance that is produced by the fetal lungs and helps to reduce surface tension in the alveoli, which allows for easier expansion and prevents lung collapse.\n3. Surfactant is produced by type II pneumocytes in the fetal lungs.\n4. The correct answer choice is D) Type II pneumocytes, as these cells are responsible for producing surfactant and are stimulated by betamethasone.",
