                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what preterm labor is?\n\nProfessor: Preterm labor is when a woman goes into labor before 37 weeks of pregnancy.\n\nStudent: What is a tocolytic?\n\nProfessor: A tocolytic is a medication that is used to stop or slow down contractions in order to delay delivery.\n\nStudent: What is betamethasone?\n\nProfessor: Betamethasone is a steroid medication that is given to pregnant women who are at risk of preterm delivery. It helps to mature the baby's lungs and reduce the risk of respiratory distress syndrome.\n\nStudent: How does betamethasone work?\n\nProfessor: Betamethasone stimulates the production of surfactant in the fetal lungs. Surfactant is a substance that helps to keep the air sacs in the lungs from collapsing and sticking together. This makes it easier for the baby to breathe after birth.\n\nStudent: Which fetal cells does betamethasone stimulate?\n\nProfessor: Betamethasone stimulates type II pneumocytes in the fetal lungs to produce surfactant. So the answer is D) Type II pneumocytes.",
