                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient is a 32-year-old woman who is presenting with symptoms of early pregnancy and has a positive urine pregnancy test. She has a history of a seizure disorder treated with carbamazepine.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is carbamazepine and how does it affect pregnancy?\n2. What are the potential complications of pregnancy in women with a history of seizures?\n3. What are the potential complications of carbamazepine use during pregnancy?\n4. Which of the listed complications are associated with carbamazepine use during pregnancy?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Carbamazepine is an anticonvulsant medication used to treat seizures. It can decrease the effectiveness of hormonal contraceptives and increase the risk of unplanned pregnancy. It is also associated with an increased risk of birth defects when used during pregnancy.\n2. Women with a history of seizures are at increased risk of complications during pregnancy, including pre-eclampsia, premature labor, and fetal distress.\n3. Carbamazepine use during pregnancy has been associated with an increased risk of birth defects, including neural tube defects and craniofacial abnormalities.\n4. The listed complications are not all associated with carbamazepine use during pregnancy. However, of the listed options, the child is at greatest risk of developing neural tube defects such as meningocele due to the mother's history of carbamazepine use.",
