                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, I have a question about the case you presented. Can you explain why the woman's last menstrual period being 7 weeks ago is important?\n\nProfessor: Of course. The last menstrual period is used to estimate the gestational age of the fetus. In this case, the woman is approximately 7 weeks pregnant.\n\nStudent: I see. And why did the physician perform a urine pregnancy test?\n\nProfessor: The urine pregnancy test is a quick and easy way to confirm pregnancy. It detects the presence of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), which is produced by the placenta after implantation.\n\nStudent: Okay, that makes sense. So, which complication is the child at greatest risk of developing?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is E) Vaginal clear cell carcinoma. This is because the woman has a history of taking carbamazepine, which is known to increase the risk of this type of cancer in female offspring exposed to the drug in utero.\n\nStudent: I see. Can you explain why the other answer choices are not correct?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Renal dysplasia is a potential complication of maternal diabetes, not carbamazepine use. Meningocele is a type of neural tube defect that can occur in any pregnancy, but there is no evidence to suggest that carbamazepine increases the risk. Teeth discoloration is a side effect of tetracycline use during pregnancy, not carbamazepine. Sensorineural hearing loss is associated with congenital rubella syndrome, not carbamazepine use.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining that, professor. It's much clearer now. \n\nProfessor: You're welcome. Always feel free to ask questions if you need clarification on anything.",
