                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What condition is the 65-year-old woman at increased risk of developing if she is treated with raloxifene for her low bone mineral density?\n\nStep-by-step:\n\n1. The patient is a 65-year-old woman who is getting a bone mineral density screening.\n2. She has no children and went through menopause at age 55.\n3. Her mother died of breast cancer at age 48.\n4. She has hypertension and takes ramipril.\n5. Her T-score at the femoral neck is -2.7 SD.\n6. The physician is considering treatment with raloxifene.\n7. We need to determine what condition she may be at increased risk of developing if she takes raloxifene.\n\nThought process:\n\nRaloxifene is a medication used to treat low bone mineral density. It works by mimicking the effects of estrogen in the body, which can help prevent bone loss. However, like estrogen, raloxifene can also have some negative side effects. One of the most significant risks associated with raloxifene is an increased risk of blood clots, including pulmonary embolism. Other potential side effects include hot flashes, leg cramps, and an increased risk of stroke. Given the patient's history of hypertension, the physician may need to consider these risks carefully before prescribing raloxifene.\n\nAnswer:\n\nA) Pulmonary embolism",
