                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's derive the differential diagnosis step by step. \n\nFirst, the patient has a T-score of -2.7 SD, which indicates osteoporosis. The physician is considering treatment with raloxifene, which is used to treat osteoporosis. \n\nRaloxifene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM). SERMs are a class of drugs that act like estrogen in some tissues and like antiestrogens in others. Raloxifene is used to treat osteoporosis in postmenopausal women. \n\nRaloxifene increases the risk of venous thromboembolism (VTE), which includes deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism (PE). The risk of VTE is highest during the first 4 months of treatment. The risk of VTE is also increased in women with a history of VTE, obesity, and immobility. \n\nRaloxifene also increases the risk of hot flashes and sweating. Other side effects include leg cramps, joint pain, and headaches. \n\nSo, the correct answer is A) Pulmonary embolism.",
