                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the question asking?\nWhich complication is the 3-year-old boy with jaundice and splenomegaly at greatest risk for?\n\nLet's break down the information given in the scenario:\n- 3-year-old boy with fatigue and yellow discoloration of skin\n- Had an upper respiratory tract infection one week ago\n- Examination shows jaundice of skin and conjunctivae\n- Spleen tip palpated 2 cm below left costal margin\n- Hemoglobin concentration is 9.4 g/dl and mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration is 39% Hb/cell\n- Coombs test is negative\n- Peripheral blood smear is shown\n\nOur thought process:\n- The symptoms and physical exam findings suggest a possible diagnosis of hemolytic anemia, which is supported by the low hemoglobin concentration and high mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration.\n- The negative Coombs test suggests that the anemia is not due to an autoimmune process.\n- The spleen tip palpation suggests splenomegaly, which can occur in hemolytic anemias as the spleen tries to remove the excess red blood cells.\n- The peripheral blood smear may provide additional information about the type of anemia.\n\nAnswer: E) Splenic sequestration crisis\nBased on the information given, the 3-year-old boy is at greatest risk for a splenic sequestration crisis, which is a complication of sickle cell anemia and other hemolytic anemias. The palpable spleen tip and peripheral blood smear may suggest sickle cell anemia, and the risk of splenic sequestration crisis is increased in young children with this condition. Malaria, acute myelogenous leukemia, cholecystitis, and renal papillary necrosis are not supported by the information given in the scenario.",
