                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Jaundice is a yellow discoloration of the skin and/or eyes that is caused by a buildup of bilirubin in the blood. The spleen is an organ that filters blood and helps fight infection. Hemoglobin is a protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. Mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration (MCHC) is a measure of the concentration of hemoglobin in red blood cells.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is causing the patient's jaundice?\n2. What is the significance of the palpable spleen tip?\n3. What is the significance of the patient's hemoglobin and MCHC levels?\n4. What are the potential complications of the patient's condition?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The patient's jaundice is likely caused by an excess of bilirubin in the blood, which can be due to a variety of factors including liver disease, hemolytic anemia, or obstruction of the bile ducts.\n2. The palpable spleen tip suggests that the patient may be experiencing splenomegaly, or enlargement of the spleen. This can be a sign of a variety of conditions, including infections, blood disorders, or liver disease.\n3. The patient's low hemoglobin concentration and high MCHC suggest that he may be experiencing hemolytic anemia, a condition in which red blood cells are destroyed faster than they can be replaced. This can be caused by a variety of factors, including infections, autoimmune disorders, or genetic conditions.\n4. The patient's condition puts him at greatest risk for splenic sequestration crisis, a potentially life-threatening complication of sickle cell disease in which the spleen rapidly enlarges and traps large numbers of red blood cells, leading to a sudden drop in hemoglobin levels and potentially causing shock. Malaria is a potential complication of sickle cell disease, but is less likely in this case as the patient's Coombs test is negative. Acute myelogenous leukemia and renal papillary necrosis are not typically associated with the symptoms and laboratory findings described in this case. Cholecystitis, or inflammation of the gallbladder, can be a complication of sickle cell disease, but is less likely to be the greatest risk in this case given the patient's other symptoms and laboratory findings.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "8b519c0b-2f72-467f-85e6-36d425581e63",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "E) Splenic sequestration crisis.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "E",
