                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea on exertion, weight loss, irregularly irregular rhythm, and a grade 3/6, low pitched, rumbling, mid-diastolic murmur heard best over the apex. Diffuse wheezing and bilateral rales are present in the lung bases. A transthoracic echocardiogram shows a left-ventricular ejection fraction of 40% and a mass in the left atrium. \n\nBased on these symptoms and findings, the most likely diagnosis is atrial myxoma, which is a benign tumor of the heart that arises from the endocardium. The tumor can cause symptoms such as chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea, and weight loss. The irregularly irregular rhythm and mid-diastolic murmur are also consistent with this diagnosis. The mass seen on echocardiogram is likely the myxoma itself.\n\nTo confirm the diagnosis, a biopsy of the mass would be necessary. A biopsy of an atrial myxoma would show a mixture of cells from different germinal layers, including stellate and spindle-shaped cells, as well as mucopolysaccharide-rich extracellular matrix. Therefore, the correct answer is C) Mixture of cells from different germinal layers. \n\nThe other answer choices are not consistent with the diagnosis of atrial myxoma. A) Nonencapsulated groups of well-differentiated skeletal muscle cells would suggest a diagnosis of rhabdomyoma, which is a rare tumor of the heart that arises from cardiac muscle cells. B) Clusters of bland cells without mitotic activity would suggest a diagnosis of lipoma, which is a benign tumor of mature fat cells. D) Encapsulated cluster of mature fat cells would also suggest a diagnosis of lipoma. E) Nests of atypical melanocytes would suggest a diagnosis of melanoma, which is a malignant tumor of melanocytes.",
