                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient's symptoms and physical exam findings suggest a cardiac issue, specifically a left atrial mass. The biopsy of the mass is likely to show cells that are consistent with the location and presentation of the mass. \n\nA) Nonencapsulated groups of well-differentiated skeletal muscle cells - This would be consistent with a rhabdomyoma, a benign tumor of skeletal muscle cells. However, this is unlikely given the patient's presentation and the fact that rhabdomyomas are more commonly found in children.\n\nB) Clusters of bland cells without mitotic activity - This could be consistent with a myxoma, a benign tumor of connective tissue cells that is the most common type of primary cardiac tumor in adults. Myxomas can present with symptoms similar to those described by the patient, including chest pain, palpitations, and dyspnea on exertion.\n\nC) Mixture of cells from different germinal layers - This would be consistent with a teratoma, a tumor that contains cells from multiple germ layers (endoderm, mesoderm, and ectoderm). However, teratomas are extremely rare in the heart.\n\nD) Encapsulated cluster of mature fat cells - This would be consistent with a lipoma, a benign tumor of fat cells. However, lipomas are extremely rare in the heart.\n\nE) Nests of atypical melanocytes - This would be consistent with a melanoma, a malignant tumor of pigment-producing cells. However, melanomas are extremely rare in the heart.\n\nTherefore, the most likely answer is B) clusters of bland cells without mitotic activity, consistent with a myxoma.",
