                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: What could be causing the patient's symptoms?\n\nProfessor: Based on the information given, the patient is experiencing chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea on exertion, and weight loss. The cardiac examination shows an irregularly irregular rhythm and a grade 3/6, low pitched, rumbling, mid-diastolic murmur heard best over the apex. Diffuse wheezing and bilateral rales are present in the lung bases. The echocardiogram shows a left-ventricular ejection fraction of 40% and a mass in the left atrium. All of these symptoms and findings suggest that the patient may have a cardiac tumor.\n\nStudent: What kind of tumor could it be?\n\nProfessor: The biopsy of the mass is likely to show which of the following: A) Nonencapsulated groups of well-differentiated skeletal muscle cells, B) Clusters of bland cells without mitotic activity, C) Mixture of cells from different germinal layers, D) Encapsulated cluster of mature fat cells, or E) Nests of atypical melanocytes. Based on the patient's symptoms and findings, the most likely answer is C) Mixture of cells from different germinal layers. This is because cardiac tumors that arise from the heart itself are usually mixtures of cells from different germinal layers.",
