                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is C) Mixture of cells from different germinal layers.\n\nExplanation:\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms and physical examination findings, she likely has a cardiac tumor, which is causing her chest pain, palpitations, and dyspnea on exertion. The presence of an irregularly irregular rhythm and a mid-diastolic murmur heard best over the apex suggest that the tumor is located in the left atrium and is interfering with the normal function of the heart.\n\nThe transthoracic echocardiogram confirms the presence of a mass in the left atrium and also shows a reduced left-ventricular ejection fraction, indicating that the tumor is affecting the heart's ability to pump blood effectively.\n\nA biopsy of the mass would be necessary to determine its exact nature, but based on the patient's age and the fact that she has no history of serious medical illness, it is unlikely to be a malignant tumor. The most likely diagnosis is a myxoma, which is a benign tumor that arises from the connective tissue cells in the heart.\n\nMyxomas are composed of a mixture of cells from different germinal layers, including stellate and spindle-shaped cells, and may also contain mucopolysaccharides and other extracellular matrix components. They are typically encapsulated and may have a gelatinous or myxoid appearance on histologic examination.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Mixture of cells from different germinal layers.",
