                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nC) Primary adrenal insufficiency (also known as Addison's disease) is the most likely diagnosis in this case. The low glucose and potassium levels, along with the high insulin and low C-peptide levels, suggest that the patient is experiencing hypoglycemia due to an insulin overdose. However, the fact that the patient has a history of asthma and hospitalizations, along with his mother's type 2 diabetes, suggests that there may be an underlying endocrine disorder. Primary adrenal insufficiency can cause hypoglycemia due to a deficiency in cortisol and other hormones, and can also cause electrolyte imbalances. Glucagonoma, a rare pancreatic tumor that can cause hypoglycemia, is less likely given the patient's age and clinical presentation. Malingering, conversion disorder, and factitious disorder imposed on another are unlikely given the objective laboratory findings.",
