                    {
                        "id": "cb034459-824a-484b-ab22-3bdb0a3db010",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-09",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the patient's symptoms and medical history.\n2. Note the vital signs and physical exam findings.\n3. Consider the patient's initial diagnosis and treatment.\n4. Evaluate the ECG results.\n5. Determine which laboratory test would have the most diagnostic utility.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. The patient is experiencing chest pain and left shoulder discomfort, which are similar to his initial symptoms of acute myocardial infarction. He was diagnosed with an ST-elevation myocardial infarction and treated with alteplase 5 days ago.\n2. The patient has a pulse of 86/min, respiratory rate of 16/min, and a blood pressure of 146/90 mm Hg. Auscultation of the chest reveals no abnormal findings.\n3. The patient's initial diagnosis was an ST-elevation myocardial infarction, which was treated with alteplase.\n4. The ECG results are difficult to interpret.\n5. The laboratory test that would have the most diagnostic utility in this case is Troponin T, as it is a highly sensitive and specific marker for myocardial injury and can help confirm or rule out a diagnosis of acute myocardial infarction. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E) Troponin T.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "b4f0a003-c15a-4211-9298-157f6f5996fd",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
