                    {
                        "id": "defa63a8-b6b3-46a0-a055-34c7ea8b2fea",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-08",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Alanine aminotransferase and B) Alkaline phosphatase are liver function tests and are not directly related to the patient's current symptoms or diagnosis of acute myocardial infarction. Therefore, they are unlikely to have diagnostic utility in this case.\n\nC) Creatine kinase (CK-MB) is an enzyme that is released into the bloodstream when there is damage to the heart muscle. Elevated levels of CK-MB can indicate a recent heart attack. However, CK-MB levels can also be elevated in other conditions such as skeletal muscle injury, so it is not a specific test for myocardial infarction.\n\nD) Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme that is found in many tissues, including the heart. Elevated levels of LDH can indicate damage to the heart muscle, but like CK-MB, it is not specific to myocardial infarction.\n\nE) Troponin T is a protein that is released into the bloodstream when there is damage to the heart muscle. Troponin T is a highly specific and sensitive marker for myocardial infarction and is the preferred test for diagnosing acute myocardial infarction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E) Troponin T.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "ae974d90-0f7c-412c-a415-ae1a2e6f8289",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
