                    {
                        "id": "ce38282c-f58e-4837-918c-34213814f4e7",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Psychological defense mechanisms are unconscious strategies that individuals use to protect themselves from unpleasant emotions such as anxiety, guilt, and shame. These mechanisms can manifest in various ways, including behaviors, thoughts, and emotions.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is displacement as a psychological defense mechanism?\n2. What is dissociation as a psychological defense mechanism?\n3. What is projection as a psychological defense mechanism?\n4. What is reaction formation as a psychological defense mechanism?\n5. What is repression as a psychological defense mechanism?\n6. Which defense mechanism is most consistent with the man's behavior?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Displacement is a defense mechanism in which an individual redirects their emotions or impulses from the original source to a substitute target that is less threatening or more acceptable. For example, a person who is angry with their boss may come home and take out their frustration on their spouse.\n2. Dissociation is a defense mechanism in which an individual disconnects from their thoughts, feelings, memories, or sense of identity to avoid emotional distress. For example, a person who has experienced trauma may mentally \"check out\" during the event to avoid the pain.\n3. Projection is a defense mechanism in which an individual attributes their own unacceptable thoughts, feelings, or behaviors to someone else. For example, a person who is cheating on their partner may accuse their partner of being unfaithful.\n4. Reaction formation is a defense mechanism in which an individual expresses the opposite of their true feelings or impulses. For example, a person who is attracted to someone of the same sex may express strong anti-gay sentiments.\n5. Repression is a defense mechanism in which an individual unconsciously blocks out or forgets painful or traumatic memories or thoughts. For example, a person who was sexually abused as a child may have no conscious memory of the event.\n6. The man's behavior is most consistent with projection, as he is accusing his wife of behavior that he himself is engaging in (flirting with co-workers).",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "1762b659-8876-42da-99ae-6f200f04aa00",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Projection.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
