                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the patient's chief complaint and medical history.\n- The patient's complaint is a missed menstrual cycle for 4 weeks.\n- She has a history of two uncomplicated and normal vaginal deliveries at term.\n\nStep 2: Review the patient's current symptoms and test results.\n- The patient does not have any other complaints currently.\n- The urine pregnancy test result is positive.\n\nStep 3: Determine the patient's desire for pregnancy continuation.\n- The patient expresses her desire to discontinue with the pregnancy and inquires about the possibility of an elective abortion.\n\nStep 4: Consider the patient's contraceptive method.\n- The patient and her partner use the withdrawal method for contraception.\n\nStep 5: Evaluate the options for elective termination of pregnancy.\n- A) Dilatation and evacuation (D&E): This procedure involves dilating the cervix and removing the contents of the uterus using suction and instruments. It is typically used for second-trimester abortions and is not appropriate for this patient.\n- B) Oxytocin-induced labor: This procedure involves inducing labor with the hormone oxytocin to deliver the fetus. It is typically used for second-trimester abortions and is not appropriate for this patient.\n- C) Manual uterine aspiration: This procedure involves using a hand-held syringe to remove the contents of the uterus. It is a safe and effective option for first-trimester abortions and is appropriate for this patient.\n- D) Dilatation and curettage (D & C) after vacuum evacuation: This procedure involves dilating the cervix and using a vacuum to remove the contents of the uterus. It is typically used for second-trimester abortions and is not appropriate for this patient.\n- E) Oral mifepristone + misoprostol: This medication regimen involves taking mifepristone to block the hormone progesterone, followed by misoprostol to induce contractions and expel the contents of the uterus. It is a safe and effective option for first-trimester abortions and is appropriate for this patient.\n\nStep 6: Choose the most appropriate option.\n- Based on the patient's gestational age and desire for pregnancy termination, the most appropriate option is C) Manual uterine aspiration or E) Oral mifepristone + misoprostol. Both options are safe and effective for first-trimester abortions. The final decision should be made in consultation with the patient and her healthcare provider.",
                        "answers": [
