                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 36-year-old woman who is gravida 3, para 2, at 42 weeks' gestation. Her pregnancy has been uncomplicated, and her two other children were born after uncomplicated vaginal deliveries at 41 and 42 weeks' gestation, respectively. Her only medication is a prenatal vitamin. She is 165 cm (5 ft 5 in) tall and weighs 86 kg (200 lb); BMI is 33 kg/m2. Her temperature is 36.8\u00b0C (98.4\u00b0F), pulse is 90/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 110/80 mmHg. Examination shows a nontender, soft uterus consistent in size with a 42-weeks' gestation.\n\nThe patient is at term and is requesting induction of labor. Her previous pregnancies were uncomplicated, and she has no medical problems. Her BMI is elevated, which increases the risk of complications during pregnancy and delivery. However, her vital signs are within normal limits, and her physical exam is unremarkable except for the size of her uterus, which is consistent with a 42-week gestation.\n\nThe child is at greatest risk for complications related to post-term pregnancy. The longer a pregnancy goes beyond 40 weeks, the greater the risk of complications. The most common complications associated with post-term pregnancy are meconium aspiration, placental abruption, and fetal distress. Polyhydramnios and caudal regression syndrome are not common complications of post-term pregnancy.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Meconium aspiration.",
