                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "it is important to note that post-term pregnancy (defined as gestational age of 42 weeks or more) is associated with increased risk of complications for both the mother and the fetus. The most common complications for the fetus include macrosomia (large birth weight), meconium aspiration, and stillbirth. \n\nOf the options given, the most likely complication for this patient's child is meconium aspiration (D). Meconium is the first stool passed by a newborn and is composed of intestinal secretions and debris. In some cases, if the fetus is stressed or in distress, it may pass meconium into the amniotic fluid before birth. If the baby inhales this meconium-stained fluid, it can cause respiratory distress and potentially lead to pneumonia or other complications. \n\nPolyhydramnios (A) is an excess of amniotic fluid and is more commonly associated with preterm labor. Acute respiratory distress syndrome (B) is a severe lung condition that can occur in premature infants or those with significant lung injury. Caudal regression syndrome (C) is a rare congenital disorder that affects the development of the lower spine and legs. Placental abruption (E) is a serious complication in which the placenta separates from the uterine wall before delivery, potentially causing fetal distress or stillbirth. While these complications can occur in post-term pregnancies, they are less likely than meconium aspiration.",
