                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Analyze the given information\n- A 28-year-old man with no known medical history has dizziness and palpitations for 2 days.\n- He attended a bachelor party where he lost several drinking games before the onset of symptoms.\n- An ECG is shown.\n\nStep 2: Interpret the ECG\n- The ECG shows a regular rhythm with a heart rate of around 150 beats per minute.\n- The QRS complex is narrow, indicating that the origin of the rhythm is above the ventricles.\n- There are no visible P waves, indicating that the atria are not contributing to the rhythm.\n- There are irregular, sawtooth-like waves in the baseline, which are consistent with atrial flutter.\n\nStep 3: Consider the possible diagnoses\n- Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (HOCM) is a genetic condition that causes thickening of the heart muscle, leading to obstruction of blood flow. It typically presents with chest pain, shortness of breath, and syncope, but not dizziness and palpitations. The ECG in HOCM may show left ventricular hypertrophy and repolarization abnormalities, but not atrial flutter.\n- Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation (PAF) is a type of irregular heart rhythm that originates in the atria. It can cause dizziness and palpitations, especially if the heart rate is fast. The ECG in PAF typically shows irregularly irregular rhythm with no discernible P waves, but not atrial flutter.\n- Brugada syndrome is a genetic condition that predisposes to ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death. It typically presents with syncope or cardiac arrest, but not dizziness and palpitations. The ECG in Brugada syndrome may show characteristic ST-segment elevation in the right precordial leads, but not atrial flutter.\n- Ventricular tachycardia (VT) is a type of fast heart rhythm that originates in the ventricles. It can cause dizziness and palpitations, especially if the heart rate is fast. The ECG in VT typically shows wide QRS complexes with a regular or irregular rhythm, but not atrial flutter.\n- Sick sinus syndrome (SSS) is a condition in which the sinus node, which normally controls the heart rate, is dysfunctional. It can cause dizziness and palpitations, especially if the heart rate is slow or irregular. The ECG in SSS may show sinus bradycardia, sinus",
