                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Look at the ECG provided.\n2. Identify any abnormalities or irregularities in the ECG.\n3. Consider the patient's symptoms and medical history.\n4. Match the ECG findings and symptoms to potential diagnoses.\n5. Choose the most likely diagnosis.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. The ECG shows a regular rhythm with a heart rate of approximately 120 beats per minute. There are narrow QRS complexes and inverted T waves in leads V1-V3.\n2. The inverted T waves in leads V1-V3 are abnormal and may indicate myocardial ischemia or infarction. The regular rhythm with narrow QRS complexes suggests a supraventricular tachycardia.\n3. The patient's symptoms of dizziness and palpitations, along with his history of heavy drinking, suggest a possible alcohol-induced arrhythmia.\n4. Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, Brugada syndrome, and sick sinus syndrome are unlikely given the ECG findings and patient history. Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation is a possibility, but the regular rhythm and narrow QRS complexes make it less likely. Ventricular tachycardia is also a possibility, but the narrow QRS complexes make it less likely than a supraventricular tachycardia.\n5. Based on the ECG findings, symptoms, and medical history, the most likely diagnosis is paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT) secondary to alcohol use. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is not listed, but the most likely diagnosis is PSVT secondary to alcohol use.",
