                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nA) Donovan bodies on Wright-Giemsa stain are most likely to be seen in this patient's condition, which is granuloma inguinale (also known as Donovanosis). This is a sexually transmitted infection caused by the gram-negative bacterium Klebsiella granulomatis. The characteristic finding on histopathology is Donovan bodies, which are intracellular inclusions within macrophages. Treatment is with antibiotics such as doxycycline or azithromycin. Gram-negative rods in school of fish formation on Gram stain are seen in infections caused by Haemophilus ducreyi, which causes chancroid. PCR positive for Chlamydia trachomatis would suggest a diagnosis of lymphogranuloma venereum, which can also present with inguinal lymphadenopathy, but typically has a different clinical presentation and is caused by a different organism. Positive RPR, VDRL, and FTA-ABS would suggest a diagnosis of syphilis, which can also present with inguinal lymphadenopathy, but typically has a different clinical presentation and is caused by a different organism. Tzanck smear with multinucleated giant cells is seen in herpes simplex virus infections.",
