                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with a painful mass in her left groin that slowly enlarged, ruptured, and ulcerated. She also had a painless ulcer on her labium majorum that resolved after a few days. These symptoms suggest a sexually transmitted infection (STI) such as chancroid or lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV).\n\nThe physical examination is significant for left-sided, tender, ulcerative lymph nodes with a dark discoloration. This finding is highly suggestive of LGV, which is caused by the bacterium Chlamydia trachomatis serovars L1, L2, and L3.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) PCR positive for Chlamydia trachomatis. Donovan bodies are seen in granuloma inguinale, which is caused by the bacterium Klebsiella granulomatis and is not commonly seen in the United States. Gram-negative rods in school of fish formation are seen in chancroid, but this condition is rare in the United States. Positive RPR, VDRL, and FTA-ABS are seen in syphilis, which can present with a painless ulcer but not with tender, ulcerative lymph nodes. Tzanck smear with multinucleated giant cells is seen in herpes simplex virus infection, which can present with painful genital ulcers but not with tender, ulcerative lymph nodes.",
