                        "cot": "The patient is presenting with a painful mass in her left groin that has enlarged, ruptured, and ulcerated. She also had a painless ulcer on her labium majorum three weeks prior, and admits to having unprotected sex with a male partner while traveling in Southeast Asia a month ago. Physical examination reveals left-sided, tender, ulcerative lymph nodes with a dark discoloration.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?\n2. What are the common causes of painful lymphadenopathy in the groin?\n3. What is the significance of the patient's travel history?\n4. What diagnostic tests can be done to confirm the diagnosis?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The most likely diagnosis for this patient is lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV), which is a sexually transmitted infection caused by the bacterium Chlamydia trachomatis. The initial painless ulcer on her labium majorum is likely the primary lesion, and the painful mass in her left groin is a result of the infection spreading to the lymph nodes.\n2. Common causes of painful lymphadenopathy in the groin include sexually transmitted infections such as chancroid, herpes simplex virus, and LGV, as well as non-infectious causes such as lymphoma and metastatic cancer.\n3. The patient's travel history to Southeast Asia is significant because LGV is more common in tropical and subtropical regions, and is often associated with travel to these areas.\n4. Diagnostic tests that can be done to confirm the diagnosis of LGV include PCR testing for Chlamydia trachomatis, serologic testing for LGV-specific antibodies, and lymph node biopsy. A Tzanck smear with multinucleated giant cells is not specific for LGV and may be seen in other viral infections. Donovan bodies on Wright-Giemsa stain are seen in granuloma inguinale, which is caused by the bacterium Klebsiella granulomatis and is not associated with LGV. Gram-negative rods in school of fish formation on Gram stain are seen in chancroid, which is also a sexually transmitted infection but is not associated with LGV. Positive RPR, VDRL, and FTA-ABS are seen in syphilis, which can also cause lymphadenopathy but is not associated with LGV.",
