                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The correct answer is B) Physostigmine.\nPhysostigmine is a drug that is used to treat the symptoms of organophosphate poisoning. It works by inhibiting the enzyme acetylcholinesterase, which is responsible for the breakdown of acetylcholine. Acetylcholine is a neurotransmitter that is involved in the regulation of muscle contraction and other functions.\nWhen organophosphates are ingested or inhaled, they bind to acetylcholinesterase and prevent it from breaking down acetylcholine. This leads to an accumulation of acetylcholine in the body, which can cause muscle spasms, blurred vision, dry mouth, and hallucinations.\nPralidoxime is another drug that is used to treat organophosphate poisoning, but it works differently than physostigmine. Pralidoxime works by displacing the organophosphate from the acetylcholinesterase enzyme, allowing the enzyme to break down acetylcholine normally.\nAtropine is a drug that is used to treat the symptoms of organophosphate poisoning, but it works differently than physostigmine. Atropine works by blocking the effects of acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors, which can help to reduce muscle spasms and other symptoms.\nPyridostigmine and neostigmine are drugs that are used to treat myasthenia gravis, a condition that is caused by the body's own immune system attacking the acetylcholine receptors on muscle cells. These drugs work by increasing the levels of acetylcholine in the body, which can help to improve muscle function in people with myasthenia gravis.",
