                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Identify the patient's symptoms and possible cause. The patient is experiencing muscle spasms, blurred vision, dry mouth, hallucinations, dry red skin, and enlarged pupils. She also mentions foraging from plants in the woods before the symptoms started. These symptoms suggest that the patient may have ingested a toxic plant.\n\nStep 2: Determine the appropriate treatment for the patient's symptoms. The patient's symptoms suggest that she may be experiencing anticholinergic toxicity, which is caused by the ingestion of plants containing anticholinergic alkaloids. The treatment for anticholinergic toxicity is to administer an anticholinesterase agent, which will increase the levels of acetylcholine in the body and counteract the effects of the toxic alkaloids.\n\nStep 3: Review the options provided in the question. The options provided are Atropine, Physostigmine, Pralidoxime, Pyridostigmine, and Neostigmine.\n\nStep 4: Determine the appropriate drug to administer. Atropine is not the appropriate drug to administer because it is an anticholinergic agent and will worsen the patient's symptoms. Pralidoxime is not the appropriate drug to administer because it is used to treat organophosphate poisoning, which is caused by exposure to certain pesticides and nerve agents. Pyridostigmine and Neostigmine are not the appropriate drugs to administer because they are used to treat myasthenia gravis, a neuromuscular disorder.\n\nStep 5: Determine the correct answer. The correct answer is Physostigmine, which is an anticholinesterase agent that is used to treat anticholinergic toxicity. Physostigmine will increase the levels of acetylcholine in the body and counteract the effects of the toxic alkaloids.",
