                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the scenario.\n- 63-year-old man with slow and steady weight gain despite attempts to control diet and increase exercise\n- Medications include pravastatin, lisinopril, and hydrochlorothiazide\n- Obese with BMI of 32 and waist circumference of 43 inches\n- Abnormal fasting blood glucose and dyslipidemia\n- Diagnosed with diabetes after oral glucose tolerance test\n\nStep 2: Recall the different types of diabetes.\n- Type 1 diabetes: autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells, leading to insulin deficiency\n- Type 2 diabetes: insulin resistance and/or impaired insulin secretion\n- Gestational diabetes: diabetes that develops during pregnancy\n- Other types of diabetes: due to genetic defects, pancreatic diseases, medications, infections, or other conditions\n\nStep 3: Consider the associations with different types of diabetes.\n- Type 1 diabetes: strong HLA class II gene makeup, auto-antibodies against pancreatic islet cell antigens\n- Type 2 diabetes: obesity, sedentary lifestyle, family history, certain ethnicities, aging\n- Other types of diabetes: specific genetic defects, pancreatic diseases, medications, infections, or other conditions\n\nStep 4: Apply the associations to the patient's scenario.\n- The patient is diagnosed with diabetes after an oral glucose tolerance test, which suggests impaired glucose tolerance or insulin resistance.\n- The patient's obesity, sedentary lifestyle, and aging are consistent with type 2 diabetes.\n- None of the other associations (HLA class II gene makeup, pancreatic islet cell amyloid deposition, pancreatic islet cell leukocyte infiltration, auto-antibodies against pancreatic islet cell antigens) are mentioned in the scenario or are relevant to type 2 diabetes.\n\nStep 5: Choose the answer that is consistent with the patient's most likely form of diabetes.\n- Answer choice E (\"Unrelated to his abdominal obesity\") is not consistent with the patient's scenario, as his obesity is a risk factor for type 2 diabetes.\n- Answer choice D (\"Auto-antibodies against pancreatic islet cell antigens\") is not consistent with the patient's scenario, as it is more commonly associated with type 1 diabetes.\n- Answer choice C (\"Pancreatic islet cell leukocyte infiltration\") and answer choice B (\"Pancreatic islet cell amyloid deposition\") are not mentioned in the scenario and are not relevant to type 2 diabetes.\n- Answer choice A (\"",
