                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 24-year-old man with chronic back pain who presents with symptoms of anxiety, nausea, abdominal cramping, vomiting, and diarrhea for three days. He denies smoking, drinking alcohol, and using illicit drugs. Physical examination shows dilated pupils, diaphoresis, and piloerection. His abdominal exam shows diffuse mild tenderness. There is no rebound tenderness or guarding. His hemoglobin concentration is 14.5 g/dL, leukocyte count is 8,000/mm, and platelet count is 250,000/mm3; serum studies and urinalysis show no abnormalities. \n\nThe symptoms and physical exam findings suggest opioid withdrawal. The patient's chronic back pain history suggests that he may have been taking opioids for pain management, and his move to Florida may have disrupted his access to these medications. \n\nThe most appropriate pharmacotherapy for opioid withdrawal is A) Lorazepam. Lorazepam is a benzodiazepine that can help alleviate symptoms of anxiety, agitation, and insomnia associated with opioid withdrawal. It does not treat the underlying opioid addiction, but it can help manage the acute withdrawal symptoms. \n\nB) Naltrexone is an opioid antagonist that can be used to treat opioid addiction, but it is not appropriate for acute withdrawal management. \n\nC) Methadone is a long-acting opioid agonist that can be used for opioid addiction treatment, but it requires specialized monitoring and management. It is not appropriate for acute withdrawal management. \n\nD) Morphine is an opioid agonist that would worsen the patient's withdrawal symptoms and is not appropriate for withdrawal management. \n\nE) Naloxone is an opioid antagonist that is used to reverse opioid overdose, but it is not appropriate for withdrawal management. \n\nTherefore, the most appropriate pharmacotherapy for this patient is A) Lorazepam.",
