                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy for a 24-year-old man with chronic back pain who presents with anxiety, nausea, abdominal cramping, vomiting, and diarrhea for three days, dilated pupils, diaphoresis, and piloerection, and diffuse mild tenderness on abdominal exam?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The patient's symptoms suggest opioid withdrawal, which is a common complication of chronic opioid use for pain management.\n- The patient denies using illicit drugs, but it's possible that he has been prescribed opioids for his chronic back pain.\n- The physical exam findings of dilated pupils, diaphoresis, and piloerection are consistent with opioid withdrawal.\n- The absence of rebound tenderness or guarding suggests that there is no acute abdominal pathology.\n- The normal hemoglobin, leukocyte, and platelet counts, as well as the absence of abnormalities on serum studies and urinalysis, suggest that there is no systemic infection or inflammation.\n- The most appropriate pharmacotherapy for opioid withdrawal is a medication that can alleviate the symptoms and prevent relapse, such as a long-acting opioid agonist (e.g. methadone) or an opioid antagonist (e.g. naltrexone).\n- Lorazepam is a benzodiazepine that can help with anxiety and agitation, but it does not address the underlying opioid withdrawal.\n- Morphine is an opioid agonist that can worsen the withdrawal symptoms and increase the risk of relapse.\n- Naloxone is an opioid antagonist that can reverse the effects of opioids, but it is not a long-acting medication and may precipitate withdrawal.\n- Therefore, the most appropriate pharmacotherapy for this patient is either methadone or naltrexone, depending on the patient's preference and clinical circumstances.\n\nAnswer: Either A) Lorazepam or C) Methadone could be correct depending on the patient's preference and clinical circumstances.",
                        "answers": [
