                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what each of the options does?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Lorazepam is a benzodiazepine that can help with anxiety and agitation. Naltrexone is an opioid antagonist that can be used to treat opioid addiction. Methadone is a long-acting opioid agonist that can be used to treat chronic pain and opioid addiction. Morphine is a short-acting opioid agonist that can be used to treat severe pain. Naloxone is an opioid antagonist that can be used to reverse opioid overdose.\n\nStudent: Why wouldn't we give him morphine for his pain?\n\nProfessor: While morphine can be effective for pain relief, it can also worsen the patient's symptoms of nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. Additionally, given his history of chronic back pain, it's possible that he has a history of opioid use, which could make him more susceptible to opioid-induced respiratory depression.\n\nStudent: So, why not give him naloxone?\n\nProfessor: Naloxone is used to reverse opioid overdose, but it wouldn't be appropriate in this case because the patient isn't currently experiencing an overdose. Additionally, naloxone can cause withdrawal symptoms in patients who are physically dependent on opioids, which could worsen his symptoms.\n\nStudent: What about naltrexone?\n\nProfessor: Naltrexone is an opioid antagonist that can be used to treat opioid addiction, but it wouldn't be appropriate in this case because the patient isn't currently seeking treatment for addiction.\n\nStudent: So, the most appropriate option is lorazepam?\n\nProfessor: Yes, lorazepam can help with the patient's symptoms of anxiety and agitation, and it doesn't have the same potential to worsen his gastrointestinal symptoms or respiratory depression as opioids do. However, it's important to note that lorazepam can also cause sedation and respiratory depression, so it should be used with caution and close monitoring of the patient's vital signs.",
