                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what lung compliance is?\n\nProfessor: Lung compliance refers to the ability of the lungs to expand and contract. It is a measure of how easily the lungs can stretch and how much effort is required to breathe.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what does it mean if there is increased lung compliance?\n\nProfessor: If there is increased lung compliance, it means that the lungs are more elastic and can expand more easily. This can be seen in conditions such as emphysema, where the lung tissue is damaged and loses its elasticity.\n\nStudent: And what about vital capacity?\n\nProfessor: Vital capacity is the maximum amount of air that can be exhaled after taking a deep breath. It is a measure of lung function and can be affected by various factors such as age, gender, and lung disease.\n\nStudent: So, if there is increased vital capacity, what does that mean?\n\nProfessor: If there is increased vital capacity, it means that the lungs are able to hold more air and can exhale more forcefully. This can be seen in athletes or individuals who engage in regular physical activity.\n\nStudent: What is residual volume?\n\nProfessor: Residual volume is the amount of air that remains in the lungs after a maximal exhalation. It is the air that cannot be forcibly expelled from the lungs.\n\nStudent: And if there is decreased residual volume?\n\nProfessor: If there is decreased residual volume, it means that there is less air remaining in the lungs after a maximal exhalation. This can be seen in conditions such as restrictive lung disease, where the lungs are unable to fully expand.\n\nStudent: What is chest wall compliance?\n\nProfessor: Chest wall compliance refers to the ability of the chest wall to expand and contract during breathing. It is a measure of how easily the chest wall can stretch and how much effort is required to breathe.\n\nStudent: And if there is increased chest wall compliance?\n\nProfessor: If there is increased chest wall compliance, it means that the chest wall is more elastic and can expand more easily. This can be seen in conditions such as obesity, where excess fat in the chest wall can make it easier to breathe.\n\nStudent: And finally, what is A-a gradient?\n\nProfessor: A-a gradient is the difference in oxygen concentration between the alveoli (air sacs in the lungs) and the arterial blood. It is a measure of how well oxygen is being transferred from the lungs to the blood.\n\nStudent: And if there is decreased A-a gradient?\n\nProfessor: If there is decreased A-a gradient, it means that there",
