                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, I have a question about the case you presented. Which drug should be administered to the patient for a medical abortion?\n\nProfessor: Good question. The drug that should be administered to this patient for a medical abortion is one of the options listed. Do you remember which ones they were?\n\nStudent: Yes, I remember. The options were Megestrol, Tamoxifen, Terbutaline, Mifepristone, and Copper intrauterine device.\n\nProfessor: Great. Now, let's go through each option and eliminate the ones that are not used for medical abortion. Megestrol is a hormone therapy used to treat breast cancer and weight loss in HIV patients. Tamoxifen is also used to treat breast cancer. Terbutaline is a bronchodilator used to treat asthma. The Copper intrauterine device is a form of long-acting reversible contraception. So, we can eliminate all of these options. That leaves us with Mifepristone.\n\nStudent: Okay, so Mifepristone is the drug used for medical abortion?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. Mifepristone is a medication that blocks the hormone progesterone, which is necessary for the pregnancy to continue. It is usually taken in combination with another medication called Misoprostol, which causes the uterus to contract and expel the pregnancy.\n\nStudent: I see. So, Mifepristone and Misoprostol are used together for medical abortion?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's right. Mifepristone is usually taken first, followed by Misoprostol a few days later. It's important to note that medical abortion is only effective up to a certain point in the pregnancy, usually up to 10 weeks. After that, a surgical abortion may be necessary.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining that, professor. I understand now.",
