                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the most likely cause of a 25-year-old woman's periodic painful double vision when looking sideways, adduction deficit in one eye, nystagmus in the other eye, and bilateral disc hyperemia?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The patient has myopia and has been wearing corrective lenses for 10 years, which is not likely to be related to her current symptoms.\n- Ten days ago, she fell off her bike and had a CT scan of the head, which showed no abnormalities. This suggests that her current symptoms are not due to acute traumatic brain injury.\n- Her only medication is an oral contraceptive, which is not likely to be related to her current symptoms.\n- Her vital signs are within normal limits, which is a good sign.\n- The pupils are equal and reactive to light, which rules out some potential causes of her symptoms.\n- Her best corrected visual acuity is 20/40 in each eye, which is not too bad.\n- She has an adduction deficit in one eye and nystagmus in the other eye when looking in the opposite direction, which suggests a problem with the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF), a pathway that connects the oculomotor nuclei and the abducens nuclei in the brainstem.\n- Fundoscopy reveals bilateral disc hyperemia, which suggests increased intracranial pressure.\n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of this patient's findings is demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus (option A). This is a classic presentation of internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO), which is caused by damage to the MLF. The bilateral disc hyperemia suggests increased intracranial pressure, which can be a complication of demyelinating diseases such as multiple sclerosis. The other options are less likely based on the patient's history and exam findings.",
                        "answers": [
