                        "cot": "\n\n1. Identify the patient's symptoms: periodic painful double vision when looking sideways.\n2. Review the patient's medical history: myopia, corrective lenses for 10 years, fell off bike 10 days ago, CT scan showed no abnormalities, takes oral contraceptive.\n3. Check vital signs: within normal limits.\n4. Conduct a physical exam: pupils are equal and reactive to light, best corrected visual acuity is 20/40 in each eye, adduction deficit in right eye and nystagmus in left eye when looking left, adduction deficit in left eye and nystagmus in right eye when looking right, bilateral disc hyperemia on fundoscopy.\n5. Consider possible causes of the patient's symptoms and findings:\n- A) Demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus: can cause internuclear ophthalmoplegia, which presents with adduction deficit in one eye and nystagmus in the other eye when looking to the side opposite the affected eye. However, this condition usually presents with other neurological symptoms, such as limb weakness or numbness, which are not present in this patient.\n- B) Compressive tumor of the pons: can cause multiple cranial nerve deficits, including ophthalmoplegia, but this would be unlikely to present with only periodic painful double vision and disc hyperemia.\n- C) Oxidative damage due to thiamine deficiency: can cause optic neuropathy and disc hyperemia, but this would not explain the patient's ophthalmoplegia and nystagmus.\n- D) Subdural hematoma extending into the orbit: can cause proptosis and ophthalmoplegia, but this would be unlikely to present with only periodic painful double vision and disc hyperemia.\n- E) Infarction of the brainstem: can cause multiple cranial nerve deficits, including ophthalmoplegia, and can present with disc hyperemia. This is the most likely cause of the patient's symptoms and findings.\n6. Choose the most likely cause: E) Infarction of the brainstem.",
