                    {
                        "id": "a81eeb1c-e6a8-4c96-887d-318991688a92",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is presenting with symptoms of temporal arteritis, which is an inflammatory condition affecting the temporal arteries. It is more common in individuals over the age of 50 and can lead to serious complications if left untreated. The diagnosis is confirmed by a temporal artery biopsy showing transmural granulomatous inflammation and fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina. Treatment with high-dose intravenous corticosteroids is necessary to prevent further complications.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is temporal arteritis?\n2. What are the symptoms of temporal arteritis?\n3. How is temporal arteritis diagnosed?\n4. What is the treatment for temporal arteritis?\n5. What are the potential complications of temporal arteritis?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Temporal arteritis is an inflammatory condition affecting the temporal arteries, which are the blood vessels that supply blood to the head and brain.\n2. Symptoms of temporal arteritis include frequent headaches, double vision, fatigue, myalgias (muscle pain), tenderness to palpation over the temples, and inward deviation of the eye.\n3. Temporal arteritis is diagnosed through a combination of physical examination, laboratory studies (such as an erythrocyte sedimentation rate), and a biopsy of the temporal artery.\n4. Treatment for temporal arteritis involves high-dose intravenous corticosteroids to reduce inflammation and prevent further damage to the arteries.\n5. Complications of temporal arteritis can include blindness, stroke, aortic aneurysm, and aortic dissection. In this case, the patient's condition puts her at greatest risk of developing aortic aneurysm or aortic dissection, which are potentially life-threatening conditions involving the aorta, the largest artery in the body.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "41732b23-9838-45f7-8ade-bd4bb63ebc9f",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
