                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nThe patient has heart failure, so he has a lot of fluid in his lungs.\nThe fluid in the lungs causes rales, which are fine crackles.\nThe patient has a systolic murmur heard best at the apex of the heart.\nThe patient has ventricular fibrillation and dies despite adequate resuscitation measures.\nThe patient has a history of myocardial infarctions.\nThe patient has a systolic murmur heard best at the apex of the heart. This means that the patient has aortic stenosis.\nAortic stenosis is caused by a build-up of calcium in the aortic valve.\nThe build-up of calcium in the aortic valve causes the valve to become stiff.\nThe valve becoming stiff causes the heart to have to work harder to pump blood.\nThe heart working harder causes the heart muscle to become thicker.\nThe heart muscle becoming thicker causes the heart to become bigger.\nThe heart becoming bigger causes the lungs to become smaller.\nThe lungs becoming smaller causes fluid to build up in the lungs.\nThe fluid in the lungs causes rales, which are fine crackles.\nThe patient has a systolic murmur heard best at the apex of the heart. This means that the patient has aortic stenosis.",
