                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient has a history of myocardial infarctions and chronic heart failure, and is presenting with symptoms of respiratory distress and cyanosis. He ultimately dies from ventricular fibrillation.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the likely cause of the patient's respiratory distress and cyanosis?\n2. What is ventricular fibrillation and how does it lead to death?\n3. What microscopic findings are associated with the patient's medical history and symptoms?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The patient's respiratory distress and cyanosis are likely due to pulmonary edema, which can be caused by heart failure. The pink sputum suggests that there is blood in the patient's lungs.\n2. Ventricular fibrillation is a type of abnormal heart rhythm in which the ventricles (the lower chambers of the heart) quiver instead of contracting normally. This can lead to a lack of blood flow to the body's organs, including the brain, and can quickly lead to death if not treated promptly.\n3. The most likely microscopic finding associated with the patient's medical history and symptoms is A) Brownish inclusions in the pulmonary macrophages on H&E staining. This finding is consistent with hemosiderin-laden macrophages, which are often seen in patients with chronic heart failure and pulmonary edema. Option B is not likely because there is no mention of cardiac tissue abnormalities on physical examination or in the patient's medical history. Option C is not likely because there is no mention of kidney symptoms or abnormalities. Option D is not likely because there is no mention of liver symptoms or abnormalities. Option E is not likely because there is no mention of amyloidosis, which is the condition associated with Congo-red staining.",
