                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what dyspnea and diaphoresis mean?\n\nProfessor: Dyspnea is difficulty breathing and diaphoresis is excessive sweating.\n\nStudent: What does it mean when the patient has a systolic murmur heard best at the apex of the heart?\n\nProfessor: A systolic murmur is a sound heard during the contraction of the heart. When it is heard best at the apex of the heart, it means that the sound is loudest at the bottom left side of the heart.\n\nStudent: What is ventricular fibrillation?\n\nProfessor: Ventricular fibrillation is a type of abnormal heart rhythm where the ventricles, the lower chambers of the heart, quiver instead of contracting properly. This can lead to a lack of blood flow to the body and can be life-threatening.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what each answer choice means?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Option A refers to brownish inclusions in the pulmonary macrophages, which are cells that help remove debris and foreign substances from the lungs. Option B refers to areas with 'ghost cells' surrounded by macrophages in the cardiac tissue, which can be a sign of previous heart damage. Option C refers to positive Prussian-blue staining of the kidney tissue, which can indicate the presence of iron. Option D refers to ground-glass hepatocytes, which are liver cells that appear cloudy and can be a sign of liver damage. Option E refers to positive Congo-red staining of the cardiac tissue, which can be a sign of amyloidosis, a condition where abnormal proteins build up in organs and tissues.\n\nStudent: So which answer choice is the correct one?\n\nProfessor: Based on the patient's symptoms and medical history, the correct answer choice would be A) Brownish inclusions in the pulmonary macrophages on H&E staining. This finding is consistent with pulmonary edema, which is a buildup of fluid in the lungs and can be a complication of heart failure.",
