                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Identify the symptoms\n- Sudden onset speech changes (distorted, random, and disconnected words)\n- Physical frustration\n- Right arm weakness\n\nStep 2: Determine the affected side of the brain\n- The patient has right arm weakness, which indicates that the left side of the brain is affected (since each side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body).\n\nStep 3: Determine the affected brain region\n- The patient's primary issue is with speech, which is primarily controlled by Broca's area (speech production) and Wernicke's area (speech comprehension). Both of these areas are located in the left hemisphere of the brain for right-handed individuals.\n- Broca's area is located in the left frontal lobe, while Wernicke's area is located in the left temporal lobe.\n\nStep 4: Choose the best answer\n- Since the patient has both speech and motor issues, it is likely that the damage is in the left frontal lobe (A), which contains both Broca's area and the primary motor cortex. Damage to the left internal capsule (B) could also cause motor issues but would not directly affect speech. Damage to the left temporal lobe (C) could affect speech comprehension but would not cause motor issues. Damage to the right frontal lobe (D) and right temporal lobe (E) would not cause the symptoms observed in this patient, as they are not involved in speech production or comprehension for right-handed individuals.\n\nAnswer: A) Left frontal lobe",
