                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the patient's symptoms: sudden onset speech changes, distorted speech with random and disconnected words, physical frustration, weakness of the right arm.\n2. Determine which side of the brain controls speech and arm movement.\n3. Identify which location on that side of the brain is responsible for the patient's symptoms.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. The patient's symptoms are sudden onset speech changes, distorted speech with random and disconnected words, physical frustration, and weakness of the right arm.\n2. The left side of the brain controls speech and arm movement.\n3. The best answer would be A) Left frontal lobe, as damage to this area can cause Broca's aphasia, which is characterized by difficulty speaking and forming coherent sentences, as well as weakness on the opposite side of the body. The left internal capsule is also a possibility, as damage to this area can cause weakness on the opposite side of the body, but it is less likely to cause speech changes. The left temporal lobe is responsible for language comprehension, but not speech production. The right frontal and temporal lobes are not responsible for speech or arm movement control.",
