                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: Which location of damage would best explain the sudden onset speech changes and weakness of the right arm in a 78-year-old right-handed man who was chatting with his daughter when his speech became distorted with random and disconnected words that made no sense?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The patient is a 78-year-old right-handed man who suddenly experienced speech changes and weakness of the right arm.\n- The speech changes are described as distorted with random and disconnected words that made no sense, and the patient seems physically frustrated that no one understands him.\n- The weakness of the right arm suggests a neurological deficit on the opposite side of the brain, which is the left side.\n- The speech changes suggest a language deficit, which is typically associated with damage to the left hemisphere of the brain in right-handed individuals.\n- The left hemisphere of the brain is responsible for language processing in most right-handed individuals, and it includes the frontal and temporal lobes as well as the internal capsule.\n- The frontal lobe is involved in language production and planning, while the temporal lobe is involved in language comprehension and recognition.\n- The internal capsule is a white matter tract that connects the frontal and temporal lobes to the rest of the brain and is important for transmitting signals between them.\n- Based on the patient's symptoms, the most likely location of damage is the left frontal lobe, which would affect his ability to produce coherent speech and express his thoughts.\n\nAnswer: A) Left frontal lobe.",
