                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The left hemisphere of the brain is responsible for language processing in most individuals, and damage to specific areas within the left hemisphere can result in different types of language deficits. The right hemisphere of the brain controls the left side of the body, and damage to the right hemisphere can result in weakness or paralysis of the left arm or leg.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What type of language deficit is the patient experiencing?\n2. Which hemisphere of the brain is likely affected?\n3. Which specific area within the affected hemisphere is responsible for language processing?\n4. Which hemisphere of the brain controls the left arm?\n5. Which specific area within the affected hemisphere is responsible for controlling the left arm?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The patient is experiencing distorted and disconnected speech that makes no sense, which is consistent with a type of language deficit called Wernicke's aphasia.\n2. Based on the patient's language deficit, it is likely that the left hemisphere of the brain is affected.\n3. Wernicke's area, located in the posterior portion of the left temporal lobe, is responsible for language comprehension and is likely the area affected in this patient.\n4. The right hemisphere of the brain controls the left arm.\n5. The specific area within the right hemisphere responsible for controlling the left arm is the left motor cortex, located in the frontal lobe. \n\nAnswer: A) Left frontal lobe. The weakness of the right arm suggests damage to the left motor cortex, which is located in the left frontal lobe. However, the patient's language deficit is consistent with damage to Wernicke's area in the left temporal lobe.",
