                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the most likely lymphatic route for ovarian cancer to metastasize to the brain in a 51-year-old Asian woman with stage 4 disease and confirmed distant metastases in the lungs and brain?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- Ovarian cancer is known to spread through the lymphatic system.\n- The lymphatic system is a network of vessels and nodes that transport lymph (a fluid containing immune cells) throughout the body.\n- Lymphatic vessels drain into lymph nodes, which filter the lymph and trap foreign substances such as cancer cells.\n- From the lymph nodes, lymphatic vessels continue to drain into larger lymphatic trunks, which eventually empty into the bloodstream.\n- The lymphatic system is divided into two main ducts: the right lymphatic duct and the thoracic duct.\n- The right lymphatic duct drains lymph from the right upper limb, right side of the thorax, and right side of the head and neck, and empties into the right subclavian vein.\n- The thoracic duct drains lymph from the rest of the body, including the left upper limb, left side of the thorax, and left side of the head and neck, and empties into the left subclavian vein.\n- Metastasis to the lungs is a common site for ovarian cancer.\n- Metastasis to the brain is less common but can occur through lymphatic spread.\n\nAnswer: \nD) Sentinel node - right lumbar trunk - cisterna chyli - thoracic duct - right subclavian vein - systemic circulation\n\nReasoning: \n- The sentinel node is the first lymph node that receives drainage from the primary tumor site.\n- The right lumbar trunk drains lymph from the right lower limb, right kidney, and right adrenal gland, and empties into the cisterna chyli.\n- The cisterna chyli is a sac-like structure at the base of the thoracic duct that receives lymph from the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdomen.\n- The thoracic duct then continues upward through the chest and empties into the left subclavian vein.\n- However, in this case, the metastasis is to the brain, which is supplied by blood from the internal carotid arteries and vertebral arteries, not the subclavian veins.\n- Therefore, the most likely lymphatic route for ovarian cancer to metastasize to the brain is through the thoracic duct, which drains into the systemic circulation and can potentially spread cancer cells to the brain via the bloodstream.",
                        "answers": [
