                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the medication mentioned in the scenario that the patient is interested in using for smoking cessation.\n2. Determine if there are any contraindications for the patient to use this medication.\n3. Evaluate the accuracy of each statement regarding the use of the medication.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. The medication mentioned is an antidepressant that efficiently helps with smoking cessation.\n2. The patient has a history of head trauma and seizure episodes, so it is important to determine if the medication has any contraindications for patients with a history of seizure disorder.\n3. \nA) Omeprazole interferes with bupropion metabolism and should be taken one hour prior to omeprazole consumption. - This statement is incorrect. Omeprazole does not interfere with bupropion metabolism, and there is no need to time the medication consumption in relation to omeprazole.\nB) Bupropion is only effective when used together with nicotine replacement products. - This statement is incorrect. Bupropion can be effective on its own for smoking cessation.\nC) Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold and it should not be taken by patients with a history of seizure disorder. - This statement is correct. Bupropion can lower the seizure threshold and should not be taken by patients with a history of seizure disorder.\nD) The physician may prescribe bupropion for this patient only after a full cardiac workup is performed. - This statement is incorrect. While bupropion can have cardiovascular side effects, a full cardiac workup is not necessary before prescribing it for smoking cessation.\nE) This patient has no contraindications for bupropion and may safely use it. - This statement is incorrect. The patient has a history of seizure disorder, which is a contraindication for bupropion use.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold and it should not be taken by patients with a history of seizure disorder.",
