                    {
                        "id": "f3202e8b-c45c-4241-a51d-39feecb8259f",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what a 15-pack-year history of smoking means?\n\nProfessor: Sure. A pack-year is a way to measure the amount of smoking someone has done over time. It's calculated by multiplying the number of packs of cigarettes smoked per day by the number of years the person has smoked. So, a 15-pack-year history means that this patient has smoked one pack of cigarettes per day for 15 years, or two packs per day for 7.5 years, or some other combination that adds up to 15 pack-years.\n\nStudent: What are nicotine replacement products?\n\nProfessor: Nicotine replacement products are medications that contain nicotine but are not cigarettes. They are used to help people quit smoking by reducing withdrawal symptoms and cravings. Examples include nicotine gum, patches, lozenges, and inhalers.\n\nStudent: What is omeprazole?\n\nProfessor: Omeprazole is a medication used to treat gastrointestinal reflux disease (GERD), which is a condition where stomach acid flows back into the esophagus and causes heartburn. It works by reducing the amount of acid produced by the stomach.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what it means that bupropion lowers the seizure threshold?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Bupropion is an antidepressant medication that has also been found to be effective for smoking cessation. However, it can lower the seizure threshold, which means that it can make it easier for someone to have a seizure. This is especially true for people who have a history of seizure disorder, like this patient.\n\nStudent: What is a cardiac workup?\n\nProfessor: A cardiac workup is a series of tests that are done to evaluate the health of the heart. This may include an electrocardiogram (ECG), echocardiogram, stress test, and other tests depending on the patient's medical history and symptoms. It's important to do a cardiac workup before prescribing bupropion because it can have effects on the heart, such as increasing heart rate and blood pressure. \n\nStudent: So, which statement is correct?\n\nProfessor: The correct statement is E) This patient has no contraindications for bupropion and may safely use it. Since the patient does not have a history of seizure disorder and is not currently taking any antiepileptic medication, bupropion is a safe option for him to try for smoking cessation. However, it's important to monitor him for any potential side effects and to make sure he is not experiencing any worsening of his GERD",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "5f32cf7a-4a92-4a47-b218-70ba86600247",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "E) This patient has no contraindications for bupropion and may safely use it.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "E",
                                "correct_answer": false
