                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Calcium oxalate crystals in urine can be a sign of kidney stones, which can cause flank pain and decreased urination. Alcoholism can also lead to kidney damage and dysfunction.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is metabolic alkalosis?\n2. What is anion gap metabolic acidosis?\n3. What is non-anion gap metabolic acidosis?\n4. What is respiratory acidosis?\n5. What is respiratory alkalosis?\n6. Which of these acid-base imbalances is most commonly associated with kidney dysfunction?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Metabolic alkalosis is a condition where there is an excess of bicarbonate in the blood, leading to an increase in pH and a decrease in acidity.\n2. Anion gap metabolic acidosis is a condition where there is an excess of acid in the blood, leading to a decrease in pH and an increase in anion gap.\n3. Non-anion gap metabolic acidosis is a condition where there is a loss of bicarbonate from the body, leading to a decrease in pH and a normal anion gap.\n4. Respiratory acidosis is a condition where there is an excess of carbon dioxide in the blood, leading to a decrease in pH and an increase in acidity.\n5. Respiratory alkalosis is a condition where there is a decrease in carbon dioxide in the blood, leading to an increase in pH and a decrease in acidity.\n6. Non-anion gap metabolic acidosis is most commonly associated with kidney dysfunction.\n\nAnswer: C) Non-anion gap metabolic acidosis is most likely to be seen in this patient, as it is commonly associated with kidney dysfunction and can be caused by conditions such as kidney stones. The presence of calcium oxalate crystals in the urine supports this diagnosis.",
                        "answers": [
