                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, let's define what a type 1 error is. A type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is true, but is rejected in error. In other words, it is a false positive result.\n\nNow, let's look at the answer choices:\n\nA) This statement is not accurate. A type 1 error does not necessarily mean the study is not significantly powered to detect a true difference between study groups.\n\nB) This statement correctly defines a type 1 error.\n\nC) This statement is not accurate. A type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is true, yet is rejected in error.\n\nD) This statement is not accurate. A type 1 error is not a beta (\u03b2) error and is not usually 0.1 or 0.2.\n\nE) This statement is not accurate. A type 1 error is not dependent on the confidence interval of a study.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) A type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is true but is rejected in error.",
