                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the symptoms and physical examination findings.\n- Sudden drooping of the right side of the face with drooling and excessive tearing\n- Inability to whistle or close the right eye\n- Circular red rash with central clearing on the trunk\n- Decreased taste sensation\n\nStep 2: Consider the patient's recent travel history.\n- The patient was recently in northern Maine and spent most of her time outdoors.\n\nStep 3: Identify the potential vectors for the organism responsible for the patient's illness.\n- Ixodes scapularis (black-legged tick) is known to transmit Lyme disease, which can cause facial nerve palsy (Bell's palsy) and a circular rash (erythema migrans).\n- Tsetse flies are found in sub-Saharan Africa and can transmit African trypanosomiasis (sleeping sickness), which does not typically cause facial nerve palsy or a circular rash.\n- Culicidae (mosquitoes) can transmit a variety of diseases, but none of them typically cause facial nerve palsy or a circular rash.\n- Dermacentor variabilis (American dog tick) is known to transmit Rocky Mountain spotted fever, which can cause facial nerve palsy and a spotted rash (not a circular rash).\n- Anopheles mosquitoes are known to transmit malaria, which does not typically cause facial nerve palsy or a circular rash.\n\nStep 4: Choose the most likely vector based on the symptoms, physical examination findings, and travel history.\n- The most likely vector is Ixodes scapularis, which is known to transmit Lyme disease and can cause facial nerve palsy and a circular rash (erythema migrans). The patient's symptoms and physical examination findings are consistent with Lyme disease, and the patient's recent travel history to northern Maine puts her at risk for tick bites.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) Ixodes scapularis.",
