                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is a 60-year-old woman with severe vulvar itching that has been gradually worsening for the past year. She has tried over-the-counter lubricants, but they do not provide substantial relief. Her medical history is remarkable for diet-controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension treated with hydrochlorothiazide. Menopause occurred at 52 years of age. She has been sexually active with her husband until recently and reports severe pain during vaginal intercourse. The physical examination reveals dry, thin, white plaque-like lesions in the vulvar area with atrophy of the labia minora. The clitoris appears retracted. The perianal skin appears pale and wrinkled. \n\nThe most likely diagnosis is B) Lichen sclerosus. Lichen sclerosus is a chronic inflammatory skin condition that affects the vulva and perianal area. It is more common in postmenopausal women and can cause severe itching, pain during intercourse, and white, thin, plaque-like lesions. Atrophy of the labia minora and retraction of the clitoris are also common findings. The condition is not related to squamous cell hyperplasia or squamous cell carcinoma of the vulva, which are both types of cancer. Lichen planus can also cause vulvar itching and white, thin, plaque-like lesions, but it is less common than lichen sclerosus. Atrophic vaginitis can cause vaginal dryness and itching, but it does not typically cause the white, thin, plaque-like lesions seen in lichen sclerosus.",
