                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The vulva is the external female genitalia, which includes the labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, and vaginal opening. Vulvar itching can be caused by a variety of conditions, including infections, skin disorders, and hormonal changes. Menopause can cause changes in the vulva due to decreased estrogen levels, leading to thinning and dryness of the skin.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the symptoms reported by the patient?\n- Severe vulvar itching\n- Pain during vaginal intercourse\n2. What is the patient's medical history?\n- Diet-controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus\n- Hypertension treated with hydrochlorothiazide\n- Menopause at 52 years of age\n3. What are the findings on physical examination?\n- Dry, thin, white plaque-like lesions in the vulvar area\n- Atrophy of the labia minora\n- Retracted clitoris\n- Pale and wrinkled perianal skin\n\nAnswer: B) Lichen sclerosus is the most likely diagnosis. Lichen sclerosus is a chronic inflammatory skin condition that commonly affects the vulva. It is more common in postmenopausal women and can cause itching, pain, and changes in the appearance of the vulva. The dry, thin, white plaque-like lesions and atrophy of the labia minora seen on physical examination are characteristic of lichen sclerosus. The retracted clitoris and pale, wrinkled perianal skin are also consistent with this diagnosis. Squamous cell hyperplasia, squamous cell carcinoma of the vulva, lichen planus, and atrophic vaginitis are less likely diagnoses based on the patient's symptoms and physical examination findings.",
