                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Diabetes and poorly controlled hypertension are risk factors for developing lower extremity swelling, also known as edema. Some medications can also cause edema as a side effect.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the mechanism of action of enalapril and metformin?\n2. What is the goal blood pressure for patients with hypertension?\n3. What are the common side effects of anti-hypertensive medications?\n4. Which anti-hypertensive medications are known to cause edema as a side effect?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Enalapril is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor that works by relaxing blood vessels and reducing blood pressure. Metformin is an oral medication used to treat type 2 diabetes by decreasing glucose production in the liver and improving insulin sensitivity.\n2. The goal blood pressure for patients with hypertension is typically less than 130/80 mmHg.\n3. Common side effects of anti-hypertensive medications include dizziness, fatigue, headache, and nausea.\n4. Nifedipine and verapamil are calcium channel blockers that can cause edema as a side effect. Hydrochlorothiazide is a diuretic that can also cause edema, but this is less common. Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic that is often used to treat edema, but it is not typically used as a first-line anti-hypertensive medication.\n\nAnswer: C) Nifedipine was likely prescribed to this patient.",
