                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is A) The oncotic pressure of the fluid leaving the capillaries is less than that of fluid entering it.\n\nTo understand why this statement is not true, we need to review the function of glomerular capillaries. Glomerular capillaries are specialized blood vessels in the kidneys that filter blood to form urine. Blood enters the glomerular capillaries through the afferent arteriole and exits through the efferent arteriole. The pressure of the blood in the capillaries forces water and small solutes, such as glucose, into the Bowman's capsule, which is the beginning of the renal tubule. This process is called glomerular filtration.\n\nNow let's examine each statement:\n\nA) The oncotic pressure of the fluid leaving the capillaries is less than that of fluid entering it.\n\nThis statement is not true. Oncotic pressure is the pressure exerted by proteins in the blood, such as albumin. The oncotic pressure in the glomerular capillaries is higher than that in the Bowman's capsule, which causes water to move from the Bowman's capsule back into the capillaries. This process is called reabsorption. Therefore, the oncotic pressure of the fluid leaving the capillaries is higher than that of the fluid entering it.\n\nB) Glucose concentration in the capillaries is the same as that in glomerular filtrate.\n\nThis statement is true. Glucose is a small solute that is filtered by the glomerular capillaries. The concentration of glucose in the capillaries is the same as that in the glomerular filtrate.\n\nC) Constriction of afferent arteriole decreases the blood flow to the glomerulus.\n\nThis statement is true. The afferent arteriole supplies blood to the glomerular capillaries. Constriction of the afferent arteriole decreases the blood flow to the glomerulus, which decreases the glomerular filtration rate.\n\nD) Hematocrit of the fluid leaving the capillaries is less than that of the fluid entering it.\n\nThis statement is true. Hematocrit is the percentage of red blood cells in the blood. During glomerular filtration, red blood cells are too large to pass through the capillary walls and are not filtered. Therefore, the hematocrit of the fluid leaving the capillaries is lower than that of the fluid entering it.",
