                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the characteristics of glomerular capillaries.\n2. Determine the oncotic pressure of the fluid leaving and entering the capillaries.\n3. Compare the glucose concentration in the capillaries and glomerular filtrate.\n4. Determine the effect of constriction of afferent arteriole on blood flow to the glomerulus.\n5. Determine the hematocrit of the fluid leaving and entering the capillaries.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. Glomerular capillaries are specialized blood vessels in the kidneys that filter blood to form urine.\n2. A) Not true. The oncotic pressure of the fluid leaving the capillaries is greater than that of fluid entering it. This is because the capillaries filter out small molecules and ions, leaving behind larger proteins that increase the oncotic pressure of the remaining fluid.\n3. B) True. Glucose concentration in the capillaries is the same as that in glomerular filtrate because glucose is a small molecule that is not filtered out by the capillaries.\n4. C) True. Constriction of the afferent arteriole decreases blood flow to the glomerulus, which decreases the filtration rate and the amount of urine produced.\n5. D) Not true. The hematocrit of the fluid leaving the capillaries is greater than that of the fluid entering it. This is because the capillaries filter out red blood cells, which increases the hematocrit of the remaining fluid.",
