                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) The oncotic pressure of the fluid leaving the capillaries is less than that of fluid entering it - This is true, as the glomerular capillaries are highly permeable to water and small solutes, but not to larger proteins. This results in a higher oncotic pressure in the capillaries, which draws fluid back in and prevents excessive loss of proteins and other important molecules in the filtrate.\n\nB) Glucose concentration in the capillaries is the same as that in glomerular filtrate - This is true, as glucose is a small molecule that is freely filtered by the glomerulus. However, it is then reabsorbed by the proximal tubule and returned to the bloodstream, so the concentration of glucose in the urine is very low.\n\nC) Constriction of afferent arteriole decreases the blood flow to the glomerulus - This is false, as constriction of the afferent arteriole would actually decrease the diameter of the vessel and increase resistance, leading to a decrease in blood flow to the glomerulus.\n\nD) Hematocrit of the fluid leaving the capillaries is less than that of the fluid entering it - This is true, as the glomerular capillaries are highly permeable to water and small solutes, but not to larger cells like red blood cells. This results in a lower hematocrit (percentage of red blood cells in the blood) in the filtrate compared to the incoming blood.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) Constriction of afferent arteriole decreases the blood flow to the glomerulus.",
