                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Glomerular capillaries are specialized capillaries in the kidneys that filter blood to form urine. The glomerular filtrate is the fluid that is filtered out of the blood in the glomerular capillaries and enters the renal tubules.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is oncotic pressure?\n2. How does the glucose concentration in the capillaries compare to that in the glomerular filtrate?\n3. What happens to blood flow to the glomerulus when the afferent arteriole constricts?\n4. What is hematocrit?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Oncotic pressure is the pressure exerted by proteins in the blood plasma that tends to pull water into the circulatory system.\n2. B) Glucose concentration in the capillaries is the same as that in glomerular filtrate.\n3. C) Constriction of afferent arteriole decreases the blood flow to the glomerulus.\n4. Hematocrit is the percentage of red blood cells in the blood. \n\nAnswer to the original question: \nD) Hematocrit of the fluid leaving the capillaries is less than that of the fluid entering it is not true about glomerular capillaries. The hematocrit of the fluid leaving the capillaries is actually higher than that of the fluid entering it, because the red blood cells are too large to pass through the capillary walls and are therefore retained in the blood.",
