                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the question and the context.\nThe question is asking about the microscopic finding observed in a patient who presented with specific clinical features. The clinical features mentioned are flat topped violaceous papules, thinning of nails, and onycholysis. \n\nStep 2: Identify the possible answers.\nThe possible answers are:\nA) Prominent necrotic cell\nB) Suprabasal split\nC) Basal cell degeneration\nD) Acantholysis\n\nStep 3: Understand the meaning of each answer option.\nA) Prominent necrotic cell - This refers to a cell that has undergone necrosis, which is cell death due to injury or disease. \nB) Suprabasal split - This refers to a split or separation of the skin layers above the basal layer. \nC) Basal cell degeneration - This refers to the degeneration or damage of the basal cells, which are the cells at the bottom layer of the epidermis. \nD) Acantholysis - This refers to the loss of cohesion between the cells of the epidermis, resulting in the formation of a blister or erosion.\n\nStep 4: Relate the clinical features to the possible answers.\nThe clinical features mentioned in the question are flat topped violaceous papules, thinning of nails, and onycholysis. These features are characteristic of a dermatological condition called lichen planus. In lichen planus, the microscopic finding observed is acantholysis, which is the loss of cohesion between the cells of the epidermis.\n\nStep 5: Choose the correct answer.\nBased on the above analysis, the correct answer is D) Acantholysis.",
