                        "cot": "The correct answer is A) Optic neuritis.\nOptic neuritis is an inflammatory condition of the optic nerve that causes sudden loss of vision. It is most commonly associated with multiple sclerosis, but can also be caused by other conditions such as Lyme disease or autoimmune disorders.\nAnterior ischemic optic neuropathy is a condition that causes sudden loss of vision due to lack of blood flow to the optic nerve. It is most commonly seen in older individuals and is associated with cardiovascular disease.\nCMV retinitis is a viral infection of the retina that can cause loss of vision. It is most commonly seen in individuals with weakened immune systems.\nFunctional vision loss is a term used to describe a loss of vision that is not due to any structural or pathological cause. It is most commonly seen in individuals with psychological or psychiatric conditions.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "1b8895b2-a976-49cd-be14-89860a9bbb3c",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": " A) Optic neuritis.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "A",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
