                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe sudden onset of severe bilateral loss of vision with no perception of light suggests a serious condition affecting both eyes. The fact that the right eye is more affected than the left eye indicates that the problem is not purely functional or psychological. \n\nThe normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus suggest that the problem is not related to the structures of the eye itself, but rather to the optic nerve or higher visual pathways. \n\nThe ability to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed suggests that the problem is more severe in the left eye, possibly indicating a lesion in the right visual cortex. \n\nBased on these findings, the most likely diagnosis is optic neuritis, which is an inflammation of the optic nerve that can cause sudden vision loss, often more severe in one eye than the other. Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy is another possibility, but this usually affects older individuals and is associated with risk factors such as hypertension and diabetes. CMV retinitis is unlikely given the normal fundus exam. Functional vision loss is also unlikely given the severity of the symptoms and the objective findings on examination.",
                        "answers": [
