                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "it is important to note that sudden severe bilateral loss of vision with no perception of light is a medical emergency and requires urgent evaluation by an ophthalmologist. \n\nBased on the given information, the most likely diagnosis is optic neuritis. Optic neuritis is an inflammation of the optic nerve that can cause sudden vision loss, often more severe in one eye than the other. The normal pupillary reflexes and fundus exam suggest that the problem is not in the retina or the anterior segment of the eye. The ability to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed suggests a visual field defect, which is common in optic neuritis. \n\nAnterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION) is another possible diagnosis, but it typically presents with sudden painless vision loss in one eye, not both. CMV retinitis is unlikely given the normal fundus exam. Functional vision loss is a possibility, but it is a diagnosis of exclusion and should only be considered after ruling out organic causes of vision loss.",
