                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Optic neuritis: This condition typically presents with sudden, painful loss of vision in one eye, not bilateral. Additionally, there would likely be abnormal pupillary reflexes and fundus examination findings.\n\nB) Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy: This condition usually affects older individuals and presents with sudden, painless, unilateral vision loss. Fundus examination would typically show a swollen optic disc.\n\nC) CMV retinitis: This condition is more common in immunocompromised patients (e.g., HIV/AIDS) and would present with floaters, flashes, and peripheral vision loss. Fundus examination would show characteristic retinal lesions.\n\nD) Functional vision loss: This diagnosis is made when there is a discrepancy between the patient's reported visual symptoms and the objective findings on examination. In this case, the patient has sudden severe bilateral vision loss, but the rest of the examination is normal. Additionally, she is able to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed, which suggests that her vision loss may not be entirely organic.\n\nBased on the information provided, the most likely diagnosis is D) Functional vision loss.",
