                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the symptoms: sudden severe bilateral loss of vision, more so on the right side, with no perception of light.\n2. Check for other examination findings: normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus.\n3. Test for visual acuity with each eye closed: able to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed.\n4. Consider possible diagnoses based on the above information.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. Symptoms: sudden severe bilateral loss of vision, more so on the right side, with no perception of light. This suggests a problem with the optic nerve or retina.\n2. Examination findings: normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus. This rules out some possible causes such as a retinal detachment or macular degeneration.\n3. Visual acuity test: able to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed. This suggests a problem with the left eye.\n4. Possible diagnoses:\n- Optic neuritis: This is inflammation of the optic nerve and can cause sudden vision loss. However, it usually affects one eye and there may be pain with eye movement, which is not mentioned in the case.\n- Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy: This is a blockage of blood flow to the optic nerve and can cause sudden vision loss. However, it usually affects one eye and there may be pain with eye movement, which is not mentioned in the case.\n- CMV retinitis: This is an infection of the retina that can cause vision loss. However, it usually affects both eyes and there may be other symptoms such as floaters or flashes of light, which are not mentioned in the case.\n- Functional vision loss: This is when there is no physical cause for the vision loss and it is due to psychological factors. However, the fact that the patient can touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed suggests that there is a physical cause for the vision loss in the left eye.\n\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis is A) Optic neuritis.",
                        "answers": [
