                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Identify the symptoms: sudden severe bilateral loss of vision, more so on the right side, with no perception of light.\n2. Check for other symptoms: normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus.\n3. Perform a finger touch test: able to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed.\n4. Analyze the symptoms and test results to determine the most likely diagnosis.\n\nAnswer:\nBased on the symptoms and test results, the most likely diagnosis is A) Optic neuritis. Optic neuritis is an inflammation of the optic nerve that can cause sudden vision loss, often more severe in one eye than the other. The normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus suggest that the problem is not in the eye itself but in the optic nerve. The finger touch test also supports this diagnosis, as it indicates that the patient has lost vision in one eye but not the other. Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy, CMV retinitis, and functional vision loss are less likely diagnoses based on the symptoms and test results.",
