                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the most likely diagnosis for a 25-year-old woman who suddenly experiences severe bilateral vision loss, with more loss on the right side and no perception of light, but has normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus, and is able to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The sudden onset of severe bilateral vision loss suggests a problem with the optic nerves or pathways.\n- The fact that the loss is more severe on the right side and there is no perception of light suggests a more complete or severe damage to the right optic nerve or pathway.\n- The normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus suggest that the problem is not in the eye itself or the structures immediately behind it.\n- The ability to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed suggests that the problem is not in the somatosensory or motor pathways.\n- Therefore, the most likely diagnosis is optic neuritis, which is an inflammation of the optic nerve that can cause sudden vision loss, often more severe in one eye, and is commonly associated with multiple sclerosis.\n\nAnswer: A) Optic neuritis",
