                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The optic nerve is responsible for transmitting visual information from the eye to the brain. Optic neuritis is inflammation of the optic nerve that can cause sudden vision loss. Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy is a condition where the blood flow to the optic nerve is reduced, leading to vision loss. CMV retinitis is a viral infection that can cause vision loss in people with weakened immune systems. Functional vision loss is a condition where there is no physical damage to the eye or optic nerve, but the person experiences vision loss due to psychological factors.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the cause of sudden severe bilateral loss of vision?\n2. Are there any other symptoms or signs present?\n3. Is there any physical damage to the eye or optic nerve?\n4. Is the loss of vision more severe on one side?\n5. Can the patient touch the tips of their fingers with both eyes closed?\n\nAnswering the sub-questions:\n1. Sudden severe bilateral loss of vision can be caused by a variety of conditions, including optic neuritis, anterior ischemic optic neuropathy, CMV retinitis, and functional vision loss.\n2. The question states that the rest of the examination, including pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus, are normal.\n3. The question does not provide information on whether there is physical damage to the eye or optic nerve.\n4. The question states that the loss of vision is more severe on the right side.\n5. The patient is able to touch the tips of their fingers with their right eye closed but not with their left eye closed.\n\nAnswer: Based on the information provided, the most likely diagnosis is A) Optic neuritis. The sudden onset of severe vision loss, more severe on one side, and the absence of physical damage to the eye or optic nerve suggest inflammation of the optic nerve. The fact that the patient is able to touch the tips of their fingers with their right eye closed but not with their left eye closed suggests that the vision loss is not due to a functional cause. However, further testing and evaluation would be necessary to confirm the diagnosis.",
                        "answers": [
