                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nDifficult. \n\nThe sudden severe bilateral loss of vision with no perception of light suggests a serious condition affecting both eyes. The fact that the right eye is more affected than the left suggests that the problem is not purely bilateral but may be asymmetric. The normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus suggest that the problem is not in the eye itself but in the optic nerve or higher visual pathways. The ability to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed suggests that the problem is not in the motor or sensory pathways but in the visual pathways. \n\nThe most likely diagnosis is optic neuritis, which is an inflammatory condition affecting the optic nerve and causing sudden vision loss, often with pain on eye movement. It is more common in young women and is often associated with multiple sclerosis. Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy is another possible diagnosis, but it usually affects older individuals and is associated with vascular risk factors such as hypertension and diabetes. CMV retinitis is a viral infection that usually affects immunocompromised individuals and causes progressive vision loss, but it would not cause sudden bilateral vision loss. Functional vision loss is a diagnosis of exclusion and would require further testing to rule out organic causes. \n\nTherefore, the answer is A) Optic neuritis.",
