                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the case presentation.\n- 25-year-old lady\n- Sudden severe bilateral loss of vision, more so on the right side\n- No perception of light\n- Normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus\n- Able to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible diagnoses based on the key information.\n- Optic neuritis: This is inflammation of the optic nerve, which can cause sudden vision loss, especially in one eye. However, the fact that the patient has bilateral vision loss and no perception of light makes this diagnosis less likely.\n- Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy: This is a condition where the blood flow to the optic nerve is blocked, leading to sudden vision loss, often in one eye. However, the fact that the patient has bilateral vision loss makes this diagnosis less likely.\n- CMV retinitis: This is a viral infection that can cause vision loss, but it typically affects people with weakened immune systems (such as those with HIV/AIDS). The patient's age and lack of other symptoms make this diagnosis less likely.\n- Functional vision loss: This is a condition where the patient experiences vision loss without any underlying physical cause. However, the fact that the patient is able to touch the tips of her fingers with one eye closed suggests that there may be a physical cause for her vision loss.\n\nStep 3: Choose the most likely diagnosis based on the information available.\n- Based on the information provided, the most likely diagnosis is optic neuritis. While the patient's bilateral vision loss and lack of perception of light may seem to contradict this diagnosis, it is still a possibility. Optic neuritis can sometimes affect both eyes, and the lack of perception of light may be due to severe damage to the optic nerve. The fact that the patient is able to touch the tips of her fingers with one eye closed suggests that her vision loss is not due to functional causes. Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy and CMV retinitis are less likely based on the information provided.",
