                        "cot": "\n\nSalbutamol is a bronchodilator medication that is commonly used to treat asthma and other respiratory conditions. It works by relaxing the muscles in the airways, making it easier to breathe. However, like all medications, salbutamol can have some adverse effects. \n\nA) Tremors: This is a common side effect of salbutamol. It occurs because the medication can stimulate the nervous system, causing muscle tremors or shaking. \n\nB) Tachycardia: This is another common side effect of salbutamol. It occurs because the medication can stimulate the heart, causing it to beat faster than normal. \n\nC) Hypoglycemia: This is not a common side effect of salbutamol. Hypoglycemia is a condition where the blood sugar levels drop too low. Salbutamol does not affect blood sugar levels, so it is unlikely to cause hypoglycemia. \n\nD) Hypokalemia: This is a potential side effect of salbutamol. Hypokalemia is a condition where the levels of potassium in the blood drop too low. Salbutamol can cause this by stimulating the release of potassium from the cells in the body. \n\nTherefore, the answer is C) Hypoglycemia.",
