                        "cot": "Salbutamol is a bronchodilator used to treat asthma and other respiratory conditions. It can cause several side effects, including:\n\nA) Tremors: Salbutamol can cause tremors, as it stimulates the beta-2 receptors in the muscles, leading to muscle contractions.\n\nB) Tachycardia: Salbutamol can cause an increased heart rate (tachycardia) due to its stimulation of beta-1 receptors in the heart.\n\nC) Hypoglycemia: Salbutamol does not typically cause hypoglycemia (low blood sugar). In fact, it may cause hyperglycemia (high blood sugar) in some cases, as it stimulates glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis in the liver.\n\nD) Hypokalemia: Salbutamol can cause hypokalemia (low potassium levels) by promoting the uptake of potassium into cells, which can lead to a decrease in serum potassium levels.\n\nBased on this information, the correct answer is C) Hypoglycemia, as it is not a typical adverse effect of salbutamol.",
