                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the relevance of blood sugar estimation\nBlood sugar estimation is a test that measures the level of glucose in the blood. It is relevant in certain medical conditions where there is a risk of low blood sugar (hypoglycemia), such as in newborn babies. Hypoglycemia can cause brain damage and other complications if not detected and treated promptly.\n\nStep 2: Analyze each option\nA) Birth asphyxia: Birth asphyxia is a condition where the baby does not receive enough oxygen during delivery. This can lead to various complications, including hypoglycemia. Therefore, blood sugar estimation is relevant in this condition.\nB) Large for date baby: A large for date baby (also known as macrosomia) is a baby who weighs more than 4,000 grams at birth. This can be a risk factor for hypoglycemia, but blood sugar estimation may not be necessary in all cases.\nC) Baby of hypothyroid mother: Hypothyroidism is a condition where the thyroid gland does not produce enough hormones. This can affect the baby's metabolism and increase the risk of hypoglycemia. Therefore, blood sugar estimation is relevant in this condition.\nD) Rh Incompatibility: Rh incompatibility is a condition where the mother's blood type is Rh-negative and the baby's blood type is Rh-positive. This can lead to various complications, but hypoglycemia is not typically one of them. Therefore, blood sugar estimation may not be necessary in this condition.\n\nStep 3: Determine the correct answer\nBased on the analysis above, the correct answer is D) Rh Incompatibility. Blood sugar estimation may not be relevant in this condition.",
                        "answers": [
