                    {
                        "id": "450f1333-3e47-4f1f-ad64-9e7dcc9dcb41",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Blood sugar levels are an important indicator of a person's health, especially in newborns. Hypoglycemia, or low blood sugar, can cause serious health problems in infants. \n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is birth asphyxia?\n2. How does being large for date affect blood sugar levels in babies?\n3. What is hypothyroidism and how does it affect blood sugar levels in babies?\n4. What is Rh incompatibility and how does it affect blood sugar levels in babies?\n\nAnswering the sub-questions:\n1. Birth asphyxia is a condition where a baby does not receive enough oxygen during birth, which can lead to brain damage and other health problems.\n2. Being large for date (LFD) means that a baby is larger than expected for their gestational age. LFD babies may have a higher risk of hypoglycemia because they have more body mass to support with their blood sugar levels.\n3. Hypothyroidism is a condition where the thyroid gland does not produce enough hormones. This can affect a baby's blood sugar levels because thyroid hormones are important for regulating metabolism, including glucose metabolism.\n4. Rh incompatibility is a condition where a mother's blood type is Rh-negative and her baby's blood type is Rh-positive. This can lead to the mother's immune system attacking the baby's red blood cells, which can cause anemia and other health problems. Rh incompatibility does not directly affect blood sugar levels in babies.\n\nAnswer: \nBased on the sub-questions, the answer is D) Rh incompatibility. Estimation of blood sugar levels is relevant in all other conditions listed (birth asphyxia, LFD, and baby of hypothyroid mother) because these conditions can increase the risk of hypoglycemia in newborns. However, Rh incompatibility does not directly affect blood sugar levels.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "a4ac5115-e885-49c5-a637-246c51231527",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Rh incompatibility.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "D",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/30 04:17:03",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
