                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is D) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis.\n\nDapsone is a sulfone antibiotic that works by inhibiting the synthesis of folic acid in bacteria and fungi. Folic acid is essential for the growth and replication of these microorganisms. Dapsone achieves this by competing with para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA), which is a precursor for folic acid synthesis. By inhibiting the synthesis of folic acid, dapsone prevents the microorganisms from producing DNA, RNA, and proteins, which are necessary for their survival and growth.\n\nIn addition to its antimicrobial properties, dapsone also has immunomodulatory actions. It is thought to work by inhibiting the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and cytokines, which are involved in the inflammatory response. This makes dapsone useful in the treatment of autoimmune diseases such as dermatitis herpetiformis and lupus erythematosus.\n\nTherefore, the mechanism of dapsone for both bacterial and fungal infections as well as immunomodulatory actions is the inhibition of folic acid synthesis by competing with PABA.",
                        "answers": [
