                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the indications for which dapsone is used.\n- Bacterial and fungal infections\n- Immunomodulatory actions\n\nStep 2: Determine the mechanism of action for each indication.\n- Bacterial and fungal infections: Dapsone inhibits the synthesis of dihydrofolic acid by competing with para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) for the active site of dihydropteroate synthase, an enzyme involved in folic acid synthesis. This leads to a depletion of folate, which is necessary for bacterial and fungal growth and replication.\n- Immunomodulatory actions: Dapsone's mechanism of action in this indication is not fully understood, but it is thought to involve inhibition of neutrophil chemotaxis and activation, as well as suppression of T-cell proliferation and cytokine production.\n\nStep 3: Compare the mechanisms of action to the answer choices.\n- A) Inhibition of cell wall synthesis: This mechanism is typically associated with antibiotics that target bacteria, but dapsone does not directly inhibit cell wall synthesis.\n- B) Inhibition of ergosterol in cell membranes: This mechanism is typically associated with antifungal agents, but dapsone does not directly inhibit ergosterol synthesis.\n- C) Inhibition of protein synthesis: This mechanism is typically associated with antibiotics that target bacteria, but dapsone does not directly inhibit protein synthesis.\n- D) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis: This mechanism is consistent with dapsone's inhibition of dihydropteroate synthase and depletion of folate, which is necessary for bacterial and fungal growth and replication. Therefore, this is the correct answer.\n\nAnswer: D) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis",
                        "answers": [
