ible that growth factor receptors have the maximum number of genes coding for receptors in the mammalian genome.\n\nD) Odorants: Odorants are molecules that are responsible for our sense of smell. They bind to specific receptors on the surface of olfactory cells in the nose and trigger a neural response that is interpreted as a smell. While there are many different types of odorant receptors, they are not likely to have the maximum number of genes coding for receptors in the mammalian genome, as the sense of smell is not as critical to survival as other cellular processes.\n\nTherefore, based on the above reasoning, the most likely answer is C) Growth factors.",
