                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The mammalian genome is the complete set of DNA that contains all the genes necessary for the development and function of an organism. Genes code for proteins, which carry out various functions in the body. Receptors are proteins that bind to specific molecules, such as hormones or neurotransmitters, and transmit signals to the cell.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are immunoglobulin receptors?\n2. What are interleukins?\n3. What are growth factors?\n4. What are odorants?\n5. Which type of receptor is most abundant in the mammalian genome?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Immunoglobulin receptors are proteins found on the surface of B cells, which are a type of immune cell. They bind to specific antigens, such as bacteria or viruses, and trigger an immune response.\n2. Interleukins are a group of signaling molecules that are involved in the immune response. They are produced by various cells, including white blood cells, and act on other cells to regulate inflammation, cell growth, and differentiation.\n3. Growth factors are signaling molecules that promote cell growth and division. They are involved in various processes, such as embryonic development, wound healing, and tissue repair.\n4. Odorants are molecules that are detected by olfactory receptors in the nose. They are responsible for our sense of smell.\n5. The correct answer is A) Immunoglobulin receptors. The mammalian genome contains approximately 10,000 genes that code for immunoglobulin receptors, which is the largest family of receptors in the genome. This reflects the importance of the immune system in protecting the body from pathogens.",
