                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Since the figure was not provided, we cannot rely on visual cues to answer this question. Instead, we must rely on our knowledge of the wave patterns of EEF, ECG, and EMG during different stages of sleep and wakefulness.\n\nEEF (electroencephalogram) measures brain activity, ECG (electrocardiogram) measures heart activity, and EMG (electromyogram) measures muscle activity. \n\nDuring wakefulness, the EEF shows beta waves (high frequency, low amplitude), the ECG shows a regular pattern of heartbeats, and the EMG shows muscle activity. \n\nDuring NREM (non-rapid eye movement) sleep, the EEF shows slower alpha and theta waves (lower frequency, higher amplitude), the ECG shows a slower heart rate, and the EMG shows reduced muscle activity. \n\nDuring REM (rapid eye movement) sleep, the EEF shows beta waves similar to wakefulness, the ECG shows a variable heart rate, and the EMG shows almost no muscle activity (except for eye movement and breathing muscles).\n\nBased on this information, we can eliminate options C and D (wakefulness and quiet wakefulness) since they both involve beta waves in the EEF and muscle activity in the EMG. \n\nOption A (NREM sleep) is also unlikely since it involves slower waves in the EEF and reduced muscle activity in the EMG. \n\nTherefore, the most likely answer is option B (REM sleep) which involves beta waves in the EEF, variable heart rate in the ECG, and almost no muscle activity in the EMG except for eye movement and breathing muscles.",
