                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Research the different tests available for assessing intestinal malabsorption.\n2. Evaluate the accuracy and reliability of each test.\n3. Determine which test is the most specific and sensitive for detecting intestinal malabsorption.\n\nGoing through each option:\nA) Fecal fat estimation: This test measures the amount of fat in the stool, which can indicate malabsorption. It is a commonly used test for assessing malabsorption and is considered to be accurate and reliable. This option is a possible correct answer.\nB) Serum lactose levels: This test measures the level of lactose in the blood, which can indicate lactose intolerance. While lactose intolerance can cause malabsorption, it is not the most specific test for assessing intestinal malabsorption. This option is not the best answer.\nC) Serum amylase levels: This test measures the level of amylase in the blood, which can indicate pancreatic function. While pancreatic insufficiency can cause malabsorption, this test is not specific to intestinal malabsorption. This option is not the best answer.\nD) NBT-PABA test: This test measures the ability of the small intestine to absorb a specific compound. It is a specific test for assessing intestinal malabsorption, but it is not as commonly used as fecal fat estimation. This option is a possible correct answer.\n\nBased on the step-by-step procedure, options A and D are the most likely correct answers. However, without additional information about the specific case, it is difficult to determine which test is the best choice.",
