                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The best test for assessment of intestinal malabsorption needs to be able to detect the malabsorption of nutrients in the intestine. \n\nA) Fecal fat estimation is a test that measures the amount of fat in the stool. This test is useful in detecting malabsorption of fat, but it does not detect malabsorption of other nutrients such as carbohydrates or proteins.\n\nB) Serum lactose levels measure the amount of lactose in the blood. This test is useful in detecting lactose intolerance, but it does not detect malabsorption of other nutrients.\n\nC) Serum amylase levels measure the amount of amylase in the blood. This test is useful in detecting pancreatic disorders, but it does not detect malabsorption of nutrients in the intestine.\n\nD) NBT-PABA test measures the absorption of para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) in the intestine. This test is useful in detecting malabsorption of nutrients in the intestine, including carbohydrates, proteins, and fats.\n\nTherefore, the best test for assessment of intestinal malabsorption is D) NBT-PABA test.",
