                        "cot": "\n\nThe most accurate test for diagnosing rabies in a patient with hydrophobia, tearing, and altered sensorium after a dog bite is histopathological examination for Negri bodies. \n\nHere's why:\n\n- Rabies is a viral infection that affects the nervous system and is transmitted through the saliva of infected animals, such as dogs, bats, and raccoons.\n- The symptoms of rabies can include hydrophobia (fear of water), tearing, altered sensorium (confusion or disorientation), and other neurological symptoms.\n- To diagnose rabies, a sample of the patient's corneal tissue can be taken using a corneal impression technique. This involves gently pressing a sterile filter paper onto the surface of the cornea to collect cells and fluid.\n- The corneal impression sample can then be examined under a microscope for the presence of Negri bodies, which are characteristic inclusion bodies (clumps of viral proteins) that are found in the cytoplasm of infected nerve cells.\n- Histopathological examination of the corneal impression sample for Negri bodies is considered the gold standard for diagnosing rabies, as it is highly specific and sensitive.\n- Other tests, such as indirect immunofluorescence and PCR for virus, can also be used to diagnose rabies, but they may not be as accurate or reliable as histopathological examination for Negri bodies.\n- Antibodies against Rabies virus can be detected in the patient's blood, but this test is not used for diagnosis of rabies in the acute phase of the disease. It is more useful for determining if a person has been previously vaccinated against rabies or if they have developed immunity after exposure to the virus.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "e11f32d7-da61-4d3b-a793-d66c87b10868",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
