                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the gene commonly associated with congenital cataract?\n\nTo answer this question, we need to identify the gene commonly associated with congenital cataract. Here are our thoughts and reasoning:\n\n- Congenital cataract is a type of cataract that is present at birth or develops during childhood.\n- There are several genes that have been linked to congenital cataract, including PAX-6, CRYGS-3, LMX-IB, and PITX-3.\n- We need to identify the gene that is commonly associated with congenital cataract, which means it is frequently found in individuals with this condition.\n- Based on our research, PAX-6 is the most commonly indicated gene in congenital cataract. It is a transcription factor that plays a critical role in eye development and is essential for the formation of the lens.\n- Therefore, the correct answer is A) PAX-6.\n\nAnswer: A) PAX-6",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "961fc2af-6d5b-4f97-948a-b36087be017c",
