                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nJaundice with 50% direct bilirubin suggests a problem with bilirubin metabolism or excretion. \n\nA) Rotor syndrome is a rare genetic disorder that causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (elevated direct bilirubin) without liver damage. However, other LFTs are normal in this case, which is not typical of Rotor syndrome. \n\nB) Gilbert syndrome is a common genetic disorder that causes unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (elevated indirect bilirubin) without liver damage. Direct bilirubin is usually normal in this condition. \n\nC) Glucuronyl transferase deficiency (also known as Crigler-Najjar syndrome) is a rare genetic disorder that causes unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to a deficiency of the enzyme glucuronyl transferase, which is responsible for conjugating bilirubin in the liver. Direct bilirubin is usually normal in this condition. \n\nD) Primary biliary cirrhosis is a chronic liver disease that causes damage to the bile ducts, leading to cholestasis (impaired bile flow) and elevated levels of both direct and indirect bilirubin. However, other LFTs are usually abnormal in this condition, which is not the case in this scenario. \n\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis in this case is C) Glucuronyl transferase deficiency (Crigler-Najjar syndrome).",
                        "answers": [
