                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's understand the given information:\n\n1. The patient has jaundice, which means there is an excess of bilirubin in the blood.\n2. 50% of the bilirubin is direct bilirubin, which is the conjugated form of bilirubin.\n3. Other liver function tests (LFTs) are normal.\n\nNow, let's analyze each option:\n\nA) Rotor syndrome: This is a rare genetic disorder characterized by elevated levels of both direct and indirect bilirubin. However, other LFTs are usually normal in this condition. This could be a possible diagnosis.\n\nB) Gilbert syndrome: This is a genetic disorder characterized by mild, unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Since the patient has 50% direct bilirubin, this option is unlikely.\n\nC) Glucuronyl transferase deficiency: This refers to a deficiency in the enzyme responsible for conjugating bilirubin. A deficiency in this enzyme would result in an increase in indirect (unconjugated) bilirubin, not direct bilirubin. Therefore, this option is unlikely.\n\nD) Primary biliary cirrhosis: This is a chronic liver disease characterized by inflammation and destruction of the bile ducts within the liver. This condition typically presents with elevated levels of alkaline phosphatase and gamma-glutamyl transferase, which are not mentioned as normal in the given information. Therefore, this option is unlikely.\n\nBased on the given information and analysis, the most likely diagnosis is:\n\nA) Rotor syndrome",
