                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Acute viral hepatitis is a liver inflammation caused by a viral infection. It can be caused by different types of viruses, including hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E. Symptoms include fever, fatigue, jaundice, and changes in urine and stool color. LFT (liver function tests) can help diagnose and monitor the condition.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the most likely diagnosis for the patient based on the symptoms and LFT results?\nAnswer: The patient is likely to have acute viral hepatitis, but the specific type of virus is unknown.\n\n2. What are the different types of acute viral hepatitis?\nAnswer: There are five types of acute viral hepatitis: A, B, C, D, and E.\n\n3. What are the recommended investigations for ruling out acute viral hepatitis?\nAnswer: The recommended investigations for ruling out acute viral hepatitis include testing for specific viral markers, such as HBsAg, IgM antiHBC, AntiHCV, and AntiHEV.\n\nAnswer to the original question:\nBased on the sub-questions, the answer to the original question is A) HBsAg, IgM antiHBC, AntiHCV, AntiHEV. These investigations will help determine if the patient has any of the common types of acute viral hepatitis and rule out other possible causes of the symptoms.",
