                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: During embryonic development, the fertilized egg undergoes a series of cell divisions and differentiation to form the three germ layers: ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm. These germ layers give rise to all the tissues and organs in the body.\n\nImportant additional facts: The blastocyst, which is the stage of development after the fertilized egg undergoes several cell divisions, consists of an inner cell mass (ICM) and an outer layer of cells called the trophoblast. The ICM gives rise to the embryo, while the trophoblast forms the placenta.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the origin of the ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm?\n2. What is the role of the epiblast and hypoblast in embryonic development?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1: \nThe ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm are derived from the epiblast and hypoblast layers of the blastocyst.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2:\nThe epiblast is the upper layer of cells in the blastocyst that gives rise to the embryo and the three germ layers. The hypoblast is the lower layer of cells that contributes to the formation of the yolk sac, which provides nutrients to the developing embryo.\n\nAnswering the original question:\nThe 3 embryonic germ layers: ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm are derived from the epiblast and hypoblast layers of the blastocyst. Therefore, the correct answer is C) Ectoderm and mesoderm from epiblast; endoderm from hypoblast.",
                        "answers": [
