                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what the 3 embryonic germ layers are?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the 3 embryonic germ layers are the ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm. They are the primary layers of cells that form during embryonic development and give rise to all the tissues and organs in the body.\n\nStudent: And where do these germ layers come from?\n\nProfessor: They are derived from the early embryo, specifically from the inner cell mass. The inner cell mass is a group of cells that forms shortly after fertilization and eventually gives rise to the entire embryo.\n\nStudent: So, which option is the correct answer?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is C) Ectoderm and mesoderm come from the epiblast, while the endoderm comes from the hypoblast. During embryonic development, the epiblast and hypoblast are two distinct layers of cells that form the early embryo. The epiblast gives rise to the ectoderm and mesoderm, while the hypoblast gives rise to the endoderm.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what each of these germ layers gives rise to?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the ectoderm gives rise to the skin, hair, nails, and nervous system. The mesoderm gives rise to the muscles, bones, blood vessels, and reproductive organs. The endoderm gives rise to the lining of the digestive tract, respiratory tract, and other internal organs like the liver and pancreas.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining that to me. I understand it better now.",
