                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe symptoms presented by the child suggest an acute upper airway obstruction, which can be caused by various conditions such as croup, epiglottitis, or foreign body aspiration. The immediate priority is to ensure airway patency and provide general airway management, which may include oxygen supplementation, positioning, and possibly intubation or tracheostomy if necessary.\n\nOnce the airway is secured, the next step is to identify the underlying cause and provide specific treatment. In this case, the presence of inspiratory stridor and drooling of saliva suggest a possible diagnosis of epiglottitis, which is a serious bacterial infection of the epiglottis and adjacent structures.\n\nThe recommended treatment for epiglottitis includes antibiotics to cover the likely pathogens, such as IV ceftriaxone, and corticosteroids to reduce inflammation and swelling of the airway, such as dexamethasone. Nebulized racemic epinephrine may be useful in some cases of croup, but it is not indicated in epiglottitis. Anti-diphtheria toxin is not relevant in this case unless there is a specific suspicion of diphtheria infection.\n\nTherefore, the most appropriate answer is C) Corticosteroids, in addition to IV ceftriaxone and general airway management.",
