                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the recommended treatment, in addition to general airway management, for a child with high grade fever, inspiratory stridor, and swallowing difficulty with drooling of saliva for the last 4-6 hours?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- The child has high grade fever, inspiratory stridor, and swallowing difficulty with drooling of saliva.\n- These symptoms suggest an upper airway obstruction, which can be life-threatening.\n- General airway management should be initiated immediately to secure the airway and provide oxygenation.\n- In addition to general airway management, the question asks for the recommended treatment.\n- Option A, nebulized racemic epinephrine, is a bronchodilator that can help relieve upper airway obstruction by reducing inflammation and swelling. It is commonly used in croup, which presents with similar symptoms.\n- Option B, anti-diphtheria toxin, is not indicated in this case because diphtheria is a rare bacterial infection that causes a thick gray coating in the throat and can be prevented by vaccination.\n- Option C, corticosteroids, can also help reduce inflammation and swelling in the upper airway. They are often used in combination with nebulized epinephrine in croup.\n- Option D, IV ceftriaxone, is an antibiotic that is not indicated in this case because the symptoms suggest an upper airway obstruction rather than a bacterial infection.\n\nTherefore, the recommended treatment in addition to general airway management is either nebulized racemic epinephrine or corticosteroids. \n\nAnswer: C) Corticosteroids.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "34981b36-9480-4c5f-8847-f8d3e35e29fa",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
