                    {
                        "id": "83c57c90-4e60-47c3-beb9-cf66966f5e40",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: What is the target sign?\n\nThe target sign is a radiological finding seen on CT or MRI scans of the brain. It is characterized by a central hypodense area surrounded by a hyperdense ring, giving the appearance of a bull's eye or target.\n\nStep 2: What is CSF rhinorrhea?\n\nCerebrospinal fluid (CSF) rhinorrhea is the leakage of CSF from the nose due to a defect in the skull base. It can be spontaneous (without any trauma) or traumatic (due to head injury).\n\nStep 3: Does the target sign occur in spontaneous CSF rhinorrhea?\n\nThere is no known association between the target sign and spontaneous CSF rhinorrhea. Spontaneous CSF leaks are usually due to a congenital or acquired defect in the skull base, and do not typically cause the characteristic radiological findings of the target sign.\n\nStep 4: Does the target sign occur in traumatic CSF rhinorrhea?\n\nYes, the target sign can be seen in traumatic CSF rhinorrhea. Trauma to the head can cause a fracture in the skull base, leading to a CSF leak. The target sign may be seen on imaging studies of the brain in such cases.\n\nStep 5: What is the answer?\n\nThe correct answer is C) Both. The target sign can be seen in traumatic CSF rhinorrhea, but not in spontaneous CSF rhinorrhea.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "c7e1b393-811e-4edd-9ed0-325ffb17e902",
