                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's understand what each term means:\n\n1. CSF Rhinorrhea: This is a condition where cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leaks from the nose due to a tear or defect in the dura mater (the outermost layer of the meninges surrounding the brain and spinal cord).\n\n2. Spontaneous CSF Rhinorrhea: This occurs without any apparent cause or trauma to the head or nose.\n\n3. Traumatic CSF Rhinorrhea: This occurs as a result of head or facial trauma, such as a skull fracture or sinus surgery.\n\nNow, let's discuss the \"Target sign\":\n\nThe \"Target sign\" is a radiological finding seen on CT scans, specifically in the context of CSF Rhinorrhea. It appears as a concentric ring pattern, with a central hyperdense area (representing blood) surrounded by a hypodense ring (representing CSF). This sign is typically associated with traumatic CSF Rhinorrhea, as it is indicative of a skull base fracture or defect.\n\nSo, based on this information, the correct answer is:\n\nB) Traumatic CSF Rhinorrhea",
