                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand what tongue thrusting is\nTongue thrusting is a habit where the tongue pushes forward against the front teeth during swallowing, speaking, or at rest. This can cause dental problems such as misaligned teeth, open bite, and speech difficulties.\n\nStep 2: Identify the features associated with tongue thrusting\nA) Spaced dentition: This is a common feature of tongue thrusting. The constant pressure of the tongue against the teeth can cause gaps or spaces between the teeth.\nB) Crowding and crossbite: This is also a common feature of tongue thrusting. The pressure of the tongue can cause the teeth to shift and become misaligned, leading to crowding and crossbite.\nC) Open bite: This is another common feature of tongue thrusting. The constant pressure of the tongue can cause the front teeth to separate, creating an open space between the upper and lower teeth.\nD) Flush terminal plane: This is not typically associated with tongue thrusting. A flush terminal plane refers to the alignment of the back teeth in the upper and lower jaws. It means that the molars on both sides of the mouth meet evenly when the jaws are closed.\n\nStep 3: Determine the answer\nBased on the above analysis, the feature that is not seen in tongue thrusting is D) Flush terminal plane.\n\nAnswer: D) Flush terminal plane",
