                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the concept of tongue thrusting\nTongue thrusting is a habit where the tongue pushes against or between the teeth during swallowing, speaking, or resting. This habit can cause dental problems such as misaligned teeth, open bite, and speech difficulties.\n\nStep 2: Analyze each option\nA) Spaced dentition: Spaced dentition refers to gaps between teeth. Tongue thrusting can cause teeth to move forward, resulting in crowded teeth, but it is unlikely to cause gaps between teeth. Therefore, option A is a possible feature of tongue thrusting.\nB) Crowding and crossbite: Crowding and crossbite are common dental problems associated with tongue thrusting. The constant pressure from the tongue can push teeth out of alignment, causing crowding or crossbite. Therefore, option B is a possible feature of tongue thrusting.\nC) Open bite: An open bite is a dental condition where the upper and lower teeth do not meet when the mouth is closed. Tongue thrusting can cause an open bite because the tongue pushes against the front teeth, preventing them from touching. Therefore, option C is a possible feature of tongue thrusting.\nD) Flush terminal plane: A flush terminal plane is a dental term that describes the alignment of the back teeth in the upper and lower jaws. It means that the last molars on both sides of the mouth meet evenly when the mouth is closed. Tongue thrusting does not affect the alignment of the back teeth, so it is unlikely to cause a flush terminal plane. Therefore, option D is not a feature of tongue thrusting.\n\nStep 3: Conclusion\nBased on the analysis, the feature that is not seen in tongue thrusting is D) Flush terminal plane.",
