                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Subcortical dementia is a type of dementia that affects the parts of the brain beneath the cortex, which is responsible for thinking, memory, and language. It is caused by damage to the subcortical structures, such as the basal ganglia and thalamus.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is Alzheimer's disease?\n2. What is Parkinson's disease?\n3. What is supranuclear palsy?\n4. What is HIV associated dementia?\n5. Which of these conditions can cause damage to the subcortical structures?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Alzheimer's disease is a progressive brain disorder that affects memory, thinking, and behavior. It is characterized by the accumulation of beta-amyloid plaques and tau protein tangles in the brain, which cause damage to the neurons and disrupt communication between brain cells.\n2. Parkinson's disease is a neurodegenerative disorder that affects movement and coordination. It is caused by the loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra, a subcortical structure that is involved in motor control.\n3. Supranuclear palsy is a rare neurological disorder that affects movement, balance, and coordination. It is caused by the degeneration of neurons in the brainstem and basal ganglia, which are subcortical structures that control movement and posture.\n4. HIV associated dementia is a type of dementia that occurs in people with HIV/AIDS. It is caused by the direct effects of the virus on the brain, as well as by the immune system's response to the infection.\n5. All of these conditions can cause damage to the subcortical structures, except for Alzheimer's disease, which primarily affects the cortex. Therefore, the answer is A) Alzheimer's disease.",
