                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " Metoprolol is a beta-blocker, which is used to treat hypertension. It works by blocking the action of certain natural chemicals in your body, such as epinephrine, on the heart and blood vessels. Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker, which is used to treat high blood pressure and certain types of chest pain. It works by relaxing the muscles of your heart and blood vessels.\n\nThe combination of Metoprolol and Verapamil can result in bradycardia with AV block, which is a slowing of the heart rate and a disruption of the normal electrical conduction of the heart. This can lead to a decrease in blood pressure and an increased risk of arrhythmias, such as atrial fibrillation and torsades de pointes. Tachycardia is not a likely result of this combination. \n\nAnswer: B) Bradycardia with AV Block",
