                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nDifficult - The question is asking about the potential result of giving Verapamil to a patient with hypertension who is already taking Metoprolol. To answer this question, we need to understand the pharmacology of these two drugs and their potential interactions. \n\nMetoprolol is a beta-blocker that works by blocking the effects of adrenaline on the heart, reducing heart rate and blood pressure. Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker that works by relaxing the blood vessels and reducing the workload on the heart. \n\nWhen these two drugs are given together, there is a potential for additive effects on heart rate and blood pressure. This can lead to bradycardia (slow heart rate) and AV block (a delay or interruption in the electrical signals that control the heartbeat). Therefore, the correct answer is B) Bradycardia with AV Block.",
                        "answers": [
