                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the question and the given information.\nThe question is asking about the possible result of giving Verapamil to a patient with hypertension who is already taking Metoprolol. Metoprolol is a beta-blocker that slows down the heart rate and reduces blood pressure. Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker that also reduces blood pressure and can slow down the heart rate.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the options.\nA) Atrial fibrillation: This is a type of irregular heartbeat that is not directly related to the combination of Metoprolol and Verapamil. It is unlikely to be the result of this combination.\nB) Bradycardia with AV Block: This is a possible result of combining Metoprolol and Verapamil because both drugs can slow down the heart rate. This option is a possible answer.\nC) Torsades De pointes: This is a type of irregular heartbeat that is usually associated with certain medications, but not specifically with Metoprolol or Verapamil. It is unlikely to be the result of this combination.\nD) Tachycardia: This is an unlikely result of combining Metoprolol and Verapamil because both drugs are known to slow down the heart rate.\n\nStep 3: Choose the correct answer.\nBased on the analysis, the correct answer is B) Bradycardia with AV Block. This is a possible result of combining Metoprolol and Verapamil because both drugs can slow down the heart rate.",
                        "answers": [
