                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the medications involved\nMetoprolol and Verapamil are both medications used to treat hypertension. Metoprolol is a beta-blocker that slows down the heart rate and reduces blood pressure, while Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker that also reduces blood pressure.\n\nStep 2: Consider the potential interactions between the medications\nCombining beta-blockers and calcium channel blockers can lead to an additive effect on heart rate and blood pressure. This means that the combination can cause the heart rate to slow down even further, potentially leading to bradycardia (slow heart rate) and AV block (a disruption in the electrical signals that control the heart).\n\nStep 3: Determine the correct answer\nBased on the potential interactions between Metoprolol and Verapamil, the correct answer is B) Bradycardia with AV Block. Atrial fibrillation, Torsades De pointes, and Tachycardia are not typically associated with this combination of medications.",
