                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the problem presented in the question.\nThe patient has altered sensorium, which means there is a change in their level of consciousness or awareness.\n\nStep 2: Determine the appropriate tests to diagnose the problem.\nThere are several tests that can help diagnose the cause of altered sensorium, including:\n\n- Complete blood counts (CBC) to check for infections or anemia\n- Random blood sugar (RBS) to check for hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia\n- Lumbar puncture (LP) to check for meningitis or encephalitis\n- Non-contrast CT head to check for bleeding or other structural abnormalities in the brain\n\nStep 3: Identify the test that should not be done.\nAll of the tests listed above are appropriate for evaluating a patient with altered sensorium, except for lumbar puncture. Lumbar puncture is contraindicated in patients with altered sensorium who have signs of increased intracranial pressure, such as papilledema or focal neurological deficits. In these cases, a CT scan should be done first to rule out any structural abnormalities before considering a lumbar puncture.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) Lumbar puncture.",
                        "answers": [
