                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "it is important to note that advanced maternal age (over 35 years old) is a risk factor for Down's syndrome. Therefore, the risk of Down's syndrome in the present pregnancy is already increased. \n\nTo assess the risk further, a combination of tests can be done. These may include:\n\n1. Maternal serum screening: This involves measuring levels of certain hormones and proteins in the mother's blood, such as alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) and human chorionic gonadotropin (HCG). Abnormal levels of these markers may indicate a higher risk of Down's syndrome.\n\n2. Ultrasound: An ultrasound can detect certain physical markers that may be associated with Down's syndrome, such as an increased thickness of the nuchal fold (the skin at the back of the baby's neck).\n\n3. Chorionic villus sampling (CVS): This involves taking a sample of the placenta to test for chromosomal abnormalities. However, this test carries a small risk of miscarriage.\n\n4. Amniocentesis: This involves taking a sample of the amniotic fluid to test for chromosomal abnormalities. This test is usually done later in pregnancy and also carries a small risk of miscarriage.\n\nThe specific tests recommended may vary depending on the individual case and the preferences of the mother and healthcare provider.",
