                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The correct answer is D) Chorionic villous biopsy.\n\nTo assess the risk of Down's syndrome in the present pregnancy, a chorionic villous biopsy is the most accurate method. This involves taking a small sample of the placenta and analyzing the chromosomes to determine if there are any abnormalities, including an extra copy of chromosome 21 which is the cause of Down's syndrome.\n\nMaterial alpha-feto protein and material HCG are both markers that can be used to screen for certain birth defects, but they are not specific to Down's syndrome. USG (ultrasound) can detect some physical abnormalities associated with Down's syndrome, but it is not a definitive diagnostic test.\n\nTherefore, the most accurate way to assess the risk of Down's syndrome in the present pregnancy is through a chorionic villous biopsy.",
                        "answers": [
