                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "How can the risk of Down's syndrome be evaluated in a current pregnancy for a 38-year-old woman who previously gave birth to a child with Down's syndrome?\n\nTo assess the risk of Down's syndrome in the present pregnancy, there are several options available. Let's evaluate each one:\n\nA) Maternal alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) - This test measures the level of AFP in the mother's blood. However, it is not a specific test for Down's syndrome and can only detect about 70% of cases.\n\nB) Maternal HCG - This test measures the level of HCG in the mother's blood. Like AFP, it is not a specific test for Down's syndrome and can only detect about 70% of cases.\n\nC) Ultrasound (USG) - This test can detect physical markers associated with Down's syndrome, such as an increased nuchal translucency (fluid buildup at the back of the baby's neck) or the presence of certain heart defects. However, it is not a definitive test and can only provide an estimate of the risk.\n\nD) Chorionic villous biopsy - This test involves taking a sample of the placenta to analyze the baby's chromosomes. It is the most accurate test for Down's syndrome, but it carries a small risk of miscarriage.\n\nBased on the options provided, the most accurate test for assessing the risk of Down's syndrome in the present pregnancy would be D) Chorionic villous biopsy. However, the decision to undergo this test should be made in consultation with a healthcare provider, taking into account the risks and benefits.",
                        "answers": [
