                        "cot_trigger": "lievin-03",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's differentiate using step by step reasoning like a medical expert.\n\n1. Obstinate cry: This is a cry that persists despite attempts to soothe the baby. It may be due to hunger, fatigue, or discomfort.\n\n2. Frighten cry: This is a cry that occurs when the baby is startled or frightened. It is usually accompanied by tears and may be followed by a period of silence.\n\n3. Hurt cry: This is a cry that occurs when the baby is in pain. It is usually accompanied by tears and may be followed by a period of silence.\n\n4. Compensatory cry: This is not a cry at all, there are no tears, no sobs, just a constant whining noise. This may be due to a need for attention or a way to manipulate the situation to get what the baby wants.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "0",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
