                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nD) Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO) is the most probable diagnosis based on the presentation and test results. CPEO is a mitochondrial myopathy that typically presents with ptosis and ophthalmoplegia, as well as other symptoms such as weakness in the facial muscles and difficulty swallowing. The absence of diplopia or visual loss, negative anti-AchR radioimmunoassay, and myopathic pattern on ERG all support the diagnosis of CPEO. Ocular myasthenia gravis and generalized myasthenia gravis can be ruled out based on the negative anti-AchR radioimmunoassay and the absence of other typical symptoms such as diplopia or muscle weakness.",
