                    {
                        "id": "f6746be1-c695-42a3-9cc1-ba9d52c0a2b5",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with ptosis, difficulty in chewing, and occasional difficulty in swallowing. These symptoms suggest a neuromuscular disorder affecting the muscles involved in these functions.\n\nOn examination, there is symmetric ptosis and mild restriction of extraocular muscle movement with finger abduction test 60deg. This finding suggests a neuromuscular disorder affecting the muscles responsible for eye movement and eyelid opening.\n\nNerve conduction study shows decremental response in orbicularis only. This finding suggests a neuromuscular junction disorder affecting the orbicularis muscle, which is responsible for eyelid closure.\n\nERG revealed a myopathic pattern. This finding suggests a muscle disorder affecting the function of the muscles involved in eye movement and eyelid opening.\n\nAnti-AchR radioimmunoassay was negative. This finding rules out the most common cause of myasthenia gravis, which is an autoimmune disorder affecting the acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction.\n\nBased on these findings, the most probable diagnosis would be chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO), which is a mitochondrial myopathy that affects the muscles involved in eye movement and eyelid opening. This condition is characterized by symmetric ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, and sometimes weakness of the facial and chewing muscles.",
                        "answers": [
