                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the main symptoms and findings in the case.\n\nThe patient presents with ptosis, difficulty in chewing, and occasional difficulty in swallowing. On examination, there is symmetric ptosis and mild restriction of extraocular muscle movement with finger abduction test 60deg. Nerve conduction study shows decremental response in orbicularis only. ERG revealed a myopathic pattern. Anti-AchR radioimmunoassay was negative.\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible diagnoses based on the symptoms and findings.\n\nThe main possibilities are myasthenia gravis (MG) and chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO).\n\nStep 3: Evaluate the evidence for each diagnosis.\n\nMG is characterized by fluctuating weakness and fatigability of skeletal muscles, including the extraocular muscles. The decremental response in the orbicularis muscle and the myopathic pattern on ERG are consistent with MG. However, the negative anti-AchR radioimmunoassay is not typical of MG, which is often associated with autoantibodies against the acetylcholine receptor.\n\nCPEO is a mitochondrial myopathy that affects the extraocular muscles and causes ptosis and ophthalmoplegia. It can also affect other muscles, including those involved in chewing and swallowing. The symmetric ptosis and mild restriction of extraocular muscle movement are consistent with CPEO. The myopathic pattern on ERG is also typical of mitochondrial myopathies.\n\nStep 4: Choose the most probable diagnosis.\n\nBased on the evidence, the most probable diagnosis is chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO). The negative anti-AchR radioimmunoassay rules out MG as the cause of the symptoms, while the findings on ERG and nerve conduction study support a myopathic disorder. CPEO is a mitochondrial myopathy that can present with ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, and other muscle weakness, and is consistent with the patient's symptoms and findings.",
                        "answers": [
