                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the patient's symptoms and examination findings.\n- Ptosis (drooping of eyelids)\n- Difficulty in chewing\n- Occasional difficulty in swallowing\n- Symmetric ptosis\n- Mild restriction of extraocular muscle movement\n- Finger abduction test 60deg\n- Decremental response in orbicularis only on nerve conduction study\n- Myopathic pattern on ERG\n- Negative anti-AchR radioimmunoassay\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible diagnoses based on the symptoms and examination findings.\n- Ocular myasthenia gravis: This is a type of myasthenia gravis that primarily affects the muscles that control eye movements. It can cause ptosis and difficulty in eye movements, but usually does not affect chewing or swallowing. The negative anti-AchR radioimmunoassay makes this diagnosis less likely.\n- Generalized myasthenia gravis: This is a type of myasthenia gravis that affects multiple muscle groups, including those involved in eye movements, chewing, and swallowing. However, the finger abduction test result of 60deg suggests mild muscle weakness, which is not typical of generalized myasthenia gravis.\n- As anti-ACHR is negative you will consider an alternative diagnosis: This is a possibility, but we need to consider other potential diagnoses first.\n- Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO): This is a rare mitochondrial disorder that affects the muscles that control eye movements, causing ptosis and ophthalmoplegia (paralysis of eye muscles). It can also affect chewing and swallowing, and the myopathic pattern on ERG supports this diagnosis.\n\nStep 3: Choose the most probable diagnosis.\nBased on the patient's symptoms, examination findings, and test results, the most probable diagnosis is chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO).",
