                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand what infective endocarditis is.\nInfective endocarditis is an infection of the inner lining of the heart chambers and heart valves. It is usually caused by bacteria or fungi that enter the bloodstream and attach to damaged heart tissue.\n\nStep 2: Identify the features of infective endocarditis.\nA) Roth spot: a retinal hemorrhage with a white center.\nB) Osler's nodes: painful, red, raised lesions on the fingers and toes.\nC) Aschoff nodules: small, inflammatory nodules in the heart tissue.\nD) Positive blood culture: the presence of bacteria or fungi in the blood.\n\nStep 3: Determine which feature is not a characteristic of infective endocarditis.\nC) Aschoff nodules are not a feature of infective endocarditis. They are actually a feature of rheumatic fever, which is a different condition that can also affect the heart.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) Aschoff nodules.",
