                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-03",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "let's define infective endocarditis (IE). IE is an infection of the endocardium, which is the inner lining of the heart chambers and heart valves. It is typically caused by bacteria or fungi that enter the bloodstream and attach to damaged heart tissue. Now, let's look at the options:\n\nA) Roth spot: This is a retinal lesion that can be seen in some cases of IE. It is caused by small emboli (clumps of bacteria or other material) that travel from the heart to the eye and block small blood vessels. The blockage causes a small area of bleeding, which appears as a red spot surrounded by a white ring.\n\nB) Osler's nodes: These are painful, tender nodules that can appear on the fingers, toes, or other parts of the body in some cases of IE. They are caused by immune complexes (clumps of antibodies and antigens) that deposit in the small blood vessels and cause inflammation.\n\nC) Aschoff nodules: These are small, granulomatous (inflammatory) lesions that can be seen in some cases of rheumatic fever, which is a complication of untreated streptococcal infections. They are not typically seen in IE.\n\nD) Positive blood culture: This is a key feature of IE, as it confirms the presence of bacteria or fungi in the bloodstream. Blood cultures are typically taken from multiple sites over a period of time to increase the chances of detecting the infecting organism.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) Aschoff nodules.",
                        "answers": [
