                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is B) Ispilateral lateral pterygoid.\n\nHere's why:\n\nThe condyle is the rounded end of the mandible (lower jaw) that articulates with the temporal bone of the skull to form the temporomandibular joint (TMJ). Lateral movement of the condyle refers to the movement of the mandible to the side, away from the midline of the body.\n\nThe lateral pterygoid muscle is one of the muscles responsible for movement of the mandible. It has two heads: a superior head and an inferior head. The superior head helps to protrude (move forward) the mandible, while the inferior head helps to retract (move backward) the mandible and also to move it laterally.\n\nWhen the inferior head of the lateral pterygoid muscle contracts unilaterally (on one side only), it pulls the condyle of the mandible forward and downward, causing lateral movement of the mandible to the opposite side. This is known as contralateral lateral movement, which is not the correct answer.\n\nHowever, when the inferior head of the lateral pterygoid muscle contracts bilaterally (on both sides), it pulls the condyles of the mandible forward and downward, causing protrusion of the mandible. Therefore, to achieve lateral movement of the mandible to the same side, the inferior head of the lateral pterygoid muscle must contract unilaterally on the same side as the movement. This is known as ipsilateral lateral movement, which is the correct answer.\n\nThe medial pterygoid muscle is another muscle responsible for movement of the mandible, but it is not involved in lateral movement of the condyle. Therefore, option C is incorrect.\n\nOption D, \"None of the above,\" is incorrect because the correct answer is B) Ispilateral lateral pterygoid.",
                        "answers": [
