                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain Ewing's postulates to me?\n\nProfessor: Sure, Ewing's postulates are a set of criteria used to determine whether a particular microorganism is the cause of a specific disease.\n\nStudent: What are the criteria?\n\nProfessor: There are four criteria. The first is that the microorganism must be present in all cases of the disease. The second is that the microorganism must be isolated from the host and grown in pure culture. The third is that the cultured microorganism must cause the same disease when inoculated into a healthy host. And finally, the microorganism must be re-isolated from the inoculated host and shown to be the same as the original microorganism.\n\nStudent: Can you give me an example of how Ewing's postulates are used?\n\nProfessor: Sure, let's say we suspect that a particular bacterium is causing a disease. We would first need to isolate the bacterium from all cases of the disease. Then we would need to grow the bacterium in pure culture. Next, we would inoculate a healthy host with the cultured bacterium and observe whether the host develops the same disease. Finally, we would re-isolate the bacterium from the inoculated host and show that it is the same as the original bacterium.\n\nStudent: I think I understand now. So, which of the options given in the question is related to Ewing's postulates?\n\nProfessor: None of the options are directly related to Ewing's postulates. However, if I had to choose one, I would say that option A, growth at the site following trauma, could potentially be related to Ewing's postulates if we suspected that a particular microorganism was causing the growth.",
