                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Additional knowledge: Hepatitis B is a viral infection that affects the liver and can cause both acute and chronic disease. It is transmitted through contact with infected blood or bodily fluids, such as through sexual contact, sharing needles, or from mother to child during childbirth. Chronic hepatitis B can lead to liver damage, cirrhosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC), a type of liver cancer.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is asymptomatic hep B?\n2. Why is asymptomatic hep B common in the normal population?\n3. What is hepatocellular carcinoma?\n4. How does chronic hepatitis B lead to HCC?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Asymptomatic hep B refers to a state where a person is infected with the hepatitis B virus but does not show any symptoms of the disease.\n2. Asymptomatic hep B is common in the normal population because the immune system is able to control the virus and prevent it from causing symptoms or liver damage in most people. However, there is still a risk of transmission and progression to chronic hepatitis B, which can lead to liver damage and HCC.\n3. Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is a type of liver cancer that originates in the hepatocytes, the main type of liver cells. It is the most common type of liver cancer and is often associated with chronic liver disease, including chronic hepatitis B.\n4. Chronic hepatitis B can lead to HCC through several mechanisms, including the integration of viral DNA with host DNA, which can disrupt normal cell growth and division and lead to the development of cancerous cells. Chronic inflammation and liver damage caused by the virus can also contribute to the development of HCC over time.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "5753200b-de07-4427-8767-53e6b99d94ba",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Integration of viral DNA with host DNA. The integration of viral DNA with host DNA can disrupt normal cell growth and division, leading to the development of cancerous cells and increasing the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "D",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
