                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nA) Glucose-6-phosphatase is the enzyme likely to be defective in this child. This enzyme is responsible for converting glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, which is necessary for glucose release from the liver during fasting. In the absence of this enzyme, glycogen accumulates in the liver, leading to hypoglycemia and other metabolic abnormalities. The fact that glucagon raises blood glucose levels after meals but not after an overnight fast is consistent with a defect in glucose-6-phosphatase, as glucagon stimulates glycogen breakdown and glucose release from the liver.",
