                    {
                        "id": "ee4a3b21-3804-4df2-971a-8aadfd263358",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe presentation of chronic liver failure and muscle weakness along with low serum glucose, ketoacidosis, and decreased pH suggests a metabolic disorder affecting glucose metabolism. \n\nThe fact that intravenous glucagon raises blood glucose levels after meals but not after an overnight fast suggests that the liver is unable to release glucose from glycogen stores during fasting. This is a characteristic feature of glycogen storage diseases (GSDs), which are a group of inherited metabolic disorders that affect glycogen metabolism. \n\nThe liver biopsy showing increased glycogen in the liver further supports the diagnosis of a GSD. \n\nAmong the four options given, glucose-6-phosphatase (G6Pase) is the enzyme responsible for the final step in gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, which is the release of glucose from glucose-6-phosphate. Deficiency of G6Pase results in GSD type I (also known as von Gierke disease), which presents with hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and hepatomegaly. \n\nTherefore, the likely enzyme defect in this child is glucose-6-phosphatase (option A).",
                        "answers": [
