                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nDifficult. \n\nThe child's symptoms suggest a glycogen storage disease. The fact that intravenous glucagon raises blood glucose levels after meals but not after an overnight fast suggests that the liver is unable to release stored glucose into the bloodstream when needed. This is a characteristic feature of glycogen storage diseases that affect the liver. The increased glycogen in the liver seen on biopsy further supports this diagnosis. \n\nThere are several types of glycogen storage diseases, each caused by a deficiency in a specific enzyme involved in glycogen metabolism. The fact that the child's liver has increased glycogen suggests that the defect is in an enzyme involved in glycogen synthesis or branching, rather than degradation. \n\nOf the options given, glucose-6-phosphatase is involved in the final step of glycogenolysis, the process by which glycogen is broken down into glucose. Deficiency in this enzyme leads to glycogen storage disease type I (also known as von Gierke disease), which is characterized by severe hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and hepatomegaly. This matches the child's symptoms, so the answer is A) Glucose-6-phosphatase.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "6874b357-1494-46cf-a8d2-c4009b226c15",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
