                    {
                        "id": "96ee8154-3412-4cec-be69-e09e5792bf59",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-05",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nDifficult. \n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\nThe serology profile mentions the following results:\n\n- HbsAg: Non-reactive\n- HBV DNA: Undetectable\n- HbeAg: Non-reactive\n- IgG Anti-HbC: Reactive\n\nThese results are related to hepatitis B virus (HBV) infection. Here is what each result means:\n\n- HbsAg: This is the surface antigen of HBV. A non-reactive result means that the patient is not currently infected with HBV.\n- HBV DNA: This is the genetic material of HBV. An undetectable result means that there is no active replication of the virus in the patient's body.\n- HbeAg: This is the e antigen of HBV. A non-reactive result means that the patient is not highly infectious.\n- IgG Anti-HbC: This is the antibody against the core antigen of HBV. A reactive result means that the patient has been exposed to HBV in the past.\n\nBased on these results, we can eliminate some options:\n\n- Chronic hepatitis inactive state: This is unlikely because the patient is not currently infected with HBV (non-reactive HbsAg) and there is no active replication of the virus (undetectable HBV DNA).\n- Chronic hepatitis recovery state: This is also unlikely because the patient is not currently infected with HBV (non-reactive HbsAg) and there is no active replication of the virus (undetectable HBV DNA).\n- Window period: This is when a person is infected with HBV but has not yet developed detectable levels of HbsAg. This is not the case here because the patient has a non-reactive HbsAg result.\n\nThe remaining option is:\n\n- Pre-core mutant infection: This is a rare form of HBV infection where the virus has mutated in a way that prevents the production of HbeAg. This can lead to a chronic infection with low levels of HBV DNA and a reactive IgG Anti-HbC result. This is the likely cause of the abnormal findings in this case.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "ee21b440-d859-4895-93d5-056e396d7341",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Pre-core mutant infection.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/15 21:11:52",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
                        "model": "{'name': 'gpt-3.5-turbo', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
